{"parentUuid":"1ff2976c-bff2-475e-80f6-c1cd797174c3","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"02b5e827-a917-46e4-a3d9-305981eb3231","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bf1dmm2Ec4uQeXJ2VAnPK2","type":"tool_result","content":"# Engine findings\n\nWhat each check against the engine proved, with the versions it ran on. The\nchecks were spikes, removed on 2026-11-09 once their findings were in the\ncode. This is a record: its sections name the phases of the plan that built\nBastion until that day, and each note describes the code of its day. Git\nhistory keeps the spikes and the old plan.\n\nVersions when the spikes ran: `iii` 0.25.0, `iii-sdk` 0.25.0, Tauri 2.12.5,\nLeptos 0.8.22, Trunk 0.22.1, rustc 1.92.0, macOS on Apple Silicon. Tauri,\nLeptos and Trunk have since left Bastion.\n\n## A0\n\n| Spike | Result | Date | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n4:checks were spikes, removed on 2026-11-09 once their findings were in the\n7:history keeps the spikes and the old plan.\n9:Versions when the spikes ran: `iii` 0.25.0, `iii-sdk` 0.25.0, Tauri 2.12.5,\n15:| Spike | Result | Date | Notes |\n21:| N5 | pass | 2026-10-30 | Compose started the `bastion-spikes` binary through `scripts.run` and restarted it: pid 66455, then 66457, in 1.4 s. |\n29:| Spike | Result | Date | Notes |\n31:| N7 | pass | 2026-10-30 | Two runs, 6.7 s and 5.9 s: `worktree::create` made a worktree of a scratch repo, its branch went into a fresh VM as a git bundle (\n35:| Spike | Result | Date | Notes |\n41:| Spike | Result | Date | Notes |\n45:| N11 | pass | 2026-10-31 | The stack's `configuration` worker (namespace `default`) can carry Bastion's harness and factory defaults (D26). `configuration::\n      63 docs/engine-findings.md\n# Bastion\n\nBastion is a software factory you can govern. A request goes in, agents work\nit through a workflow, and a reviewed change comes out. A workflow is a graph\nof steps (Bastion's own stages, skills, prompts and commands) that you see on\na canvas, and each step waits for your approval or runs on its own, as you set\nit. Bastion is a server and its clients: `bastion-server` runs the factory on\nthe [iii](https://iii.dev) engine, and the desktop client (Rust, on\n[iced](https://iced.rs)) talks to it over HTTP. It's early, and\n[DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md) holds the goals and the decisions behind it.\n\n```sh\ncargo build -p bastion-server\ncargo run -p bastion-desktop\n```\n\nThe client starts a local server if none runs on port 49190, and the server\nstarts the stack in `worker-compose.yaml`. Go to **Engine** to see Doctor and\nthe stack, then **Jobs** and **New request** to start work.\n\n## What it is not\n\nNot an editor: the only code you see in Bastion is the diff a job proposes.\nNot a web app. Not Windows, because sandbox VMs need Apple Silicon or Linux\nwith `/dev/kvm`. A server listens on loopback unless you give it a TLS\ncertificate, and then several people can share it, each with a token the\nserver issued them.\n\n## What you need\n\n- Rust, from `rust-toolchain.toml` (rustup picks it up).\n- `iii` 0.25.0 on your `PATH`.\n- A model provider key, set in **Settings**, which keeps it in the stack's\n  vault. A key in the environment works too.\n- `gh`, signed in, for pushes and pull requests. Landing locally works\n  without it.\n\nIf something is missing, Doctor on the Engine page names it and says how to\nfix it. A failed required check stops the start.\n\n## How it fits together\n\n| Part | Where | Does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core | `crates/bastion-core` | The factory's rules, as pure functions. |\n| Protocol | `crates/bastion-protocol` | The API's types and routes, shared by the server and its clients. |\n| Server | `crates/bastion-server` | The HTTP API and its events, the factory, the store, the gate, the audit and the stack. |\n| Engine adapter | `crates/bastion-server/src/engine/` | The only server code that imports `iii-sdk`. |\n| Desktop client | `crates/bastion-desktop` | The pages, on iced. |\n| Workers | `workers/` | Bastion's own iii workers. |\n\n`scripts/check-seams.sh` fails the build when code crosses a seam (DECISIONS.md\nD19).\n\nWhat the engine was found to do, and on which versions, is in\n`docs/engine-findings.md`.\n\n## Checks\n\n```sh\ncargo fmt --all -- --check\nscripts/check-seams.sh\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo test --workspace\n```\n\nCI runs the same on macOS and Linux. The live checks need a running stack:\n`cargo test -p bastion-server -- --ignored --nocapture`.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"1a284f64-c3b3-4867-981f-33384de25a5e","timestamp":"2026-11-11T17:50:24.277Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Engine findings\n\nWhat each check against the engine proved, with the versions it ran on. The\nchecks were spikes, removed on 2026-11-09 once their findings were in the\ncode. This is a record: its sections name the phases of the plan that built\nBastion until that day, and each note describes the code of its day. Git\nhistory keeps the spikes and the old plan.\n\nVersions when the spikes ran: `iii` 0.25.0, `iii-sdk` 0.25.0, Tauri 2.12.5,\nLeptos 0.8.22, Trunk 0.22.1, rustc 1.92.0, macOS on Apple Silicon. Tauri,\nLeptos and Trunk have since left Bastion.\n\n## A0\n\n| Spike | Result | Date | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n4:checks were spikes, removed on 2026-11-09 once their findings were in the\n7:history keeps the spikes and the old plan.\n9:Versions when the spikes ran: `iii` 0.25.0, `iii-sdk` 0.25.0, Tauri 2.12.5,\n15:| Spike | Result | Date | Notes |\n21:| N5 | pass | 2026-10-30 | Compose started the `bastion-spikes` binary through `scripts.run` and restarted it: pid 66455, then 66457, in 1.4 s. |\n29:| Spike | Result | Date | Notes |\n31:| N7 | pass | 2026-10-30 | Two runs, 6.7 s and 5.9 s: `worktree::create` made a worktree of a scratch repo, its branch went into a fresh VM as a git bundle (\n35:| Spike | Result | Date | Notes |\n41:| Spike | Result | Date | Notes |\n45:| N11 | pass | 2026-10-31 | The stack's `configuration` worker (namespace `default`) can carry Bastion's harness and factory defaults (D26). `configuration::\n      63 docs/engine-findings.md\n# Bastion\n\nBastion is a software factory you can govern. A request goes in, agents work\nit through a workflow, and a reviewed change comes out. A workflow is a graph\nof steps (Bastion's own stages, skills, prompts and commands) that you see on\na canvas, and each step waits for your approval or runs on its own, as you set\nit. Bastion is a server and its clients: `bastion-server` runs the factory on\nthe [iii](https://iii.dev) engine, and the desktop client (Rust, on\n[iced](https://iced.rs)) talks to it over HTTP. It's early, and\n[DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md) holds the goals and the decisions behind it.\n\n```sh\ncargo build -p bastion-server\ncargo run -p bastion-desktop\n```\n\nThe client starts a local server if none runs on port 49190, and the server\nstarts the stack in `worker-compose.yaml`. Go to **Engine** to see Doctor and\nthe stack, then **Jobs** and **New request** to start work.\n\n## What it is not\n\nNot an editor: the only code you see in Bastion is the diff a job proposes.\nNot a web app. Not Windows, because sandbox VMs need Apple Silicon or Linux\nwith `/dev/kvm`. A server listens on loopback unless you give it a TLS\ncertificate, and then several people can share it, each with a token the\nserver issued them.\n\n## What you need\n\n- Rust, from `rust-toolchain.toml` (rustup picks it up).\n- `iii` 0.25.0 on your `PATH`.\n- A model provider key, set in **Settings**, which keeps it in the stack's\n  vault. A key in the environment works too.\n- `gh`, signed in, for pushes and pull requests. Landing locally works\n  without it.\n\nIf something is missing, Doctor on the Engine page names it and says how to\nfix it. A failed required check stops the start.\n\n## How it fits together\n\n| Part | Where | Does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core | `crates/bastion-core` | The factory's rules, as pure functions. |\n| Protocol | `crates/bastion-protocol` | The API's types and routes, shared by the server and its clients. |\n| Server | `crates/bastion-server` | The HTTP API and its events, the factory, the store, the gate, the audit and the stack. |\n| Engine adapter | `crates/bastion-server/src/engine/` | The only server code that imports `iii-sdk`. |\n| Desktop client | `crates/bastion-desktop` | The pages, on iced. |\n| Workers | `workers/` | Bastion's own iii workers. |\n\n`scripts/check-seams.sh` fails the build when code crosses a seam (DECISIONS.md\nD19).\n\nWhat the engine was found to do, and on which versions, is in\n`docs/engine-findings.md`.\n\n## Checks\n\n```sh\ncargo fmt --all -- --check\nscripts/check-seams.sh\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo test --workspace\n```\n\nCI runs the same on macOS and Linux. The live checks need a running stack:\n`cargo test -p bastion-server -- --ignored --nocapture`.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"1257efdc-cb39-4a21-8a62-dc629adc8d62","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","root":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":true,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1ff2976c-bff2-475e-80f6-c1cd797174c3","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
