"-  stage's output: scope splits, discover questions, the acceptance tests,","-  UAT sign-off, learn, escapes, the CI and deploy watches.","+- Delete the built-in stages and the pipelines.","+- Delete the features that read a stage's output. These are scope splits,","+  discover questions, the acceptance tests, UAT sign-off, learn, escapes,","+  and the CI and deploy watches."," - A migration moves stored jobs and workflows to steps."," - DECISIONS.md marks each replaced decision, and the README says what","   Bastion is now."]}]}],"moreFiles":0,"changedFiles":["/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/PLAN.md"]}},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"8d16d5ae-18ac-49ad-8abb-d9ff46014727","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","root":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":0,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":1},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"8e8d0811-f97c-4fd8-abfd-3aa6c7dae713","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"5a02864f-6366-4fd2-bd48-6f892ac39809","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/PLAN.md","snippet":"1\t# Bastion plan: a workflow is its steps\n2\t\n3\t<!-- voice-register: informational -->\n4\t<!-- voice-english: ste -->\n5\t\n6\tA job runs its workflow's steps in order, and nothing else. A step is one of\n7\tfive kinds, and each kind has one text to edit. Bastion seeds the common\n8\tworkflows with that text filled in, and the owner can change all of it. The\n9\tstage concept goes away: no step has behavior that its text does not say.\n10\tThe cost is the safety the built-in stages gave. Landing, pull requests,\n11\tmerges, quality gates and acceptance tests become text the owner can read\n12\tand change, not code Bastion enforces.\n13\t\n...\n45\t  land stage accepts such a workflow.\n46\t- The workspace gets its finish setting: merge, or leave the branch.\n47\t- Exit, live: a workflow of a planning prompt, a building prompt, a test\n48\t  shell step and a network shell step that opens a pull request on\n49\t  `ianparo/bastion-demo`.\n50\t\n51\t### S2. The seeded workflows are steps\n52\t\n53\t- Each seeded workflow becomes the five kinds, with its text filled in.\n54\t- The plan and build prompts start from today's briefs, and review is a\n55\t  prompt. The pull request, the CI wait and the merge are `gh` shell scripts.\n56\t- \"Runs as\" lists only the five kinds.\n57\t- Exit, live: the seeded short workflow lands a change on `demo-local`, and\n58\t  the seeded pull request workflow opens and merges a pull request.\n59\t\n60\t### S3. Everything names steps\n61\t\n62\t- Governance per step (D42), dials per step only, notices and the job\n63\t  page by step id, evals by step.\n64\t- Exit, live: a read-only step's write is refused and noted in the audit.\n65\t\n66\t### S4. The stage code goes\n67\t\n68\t- Delete the built-in stages and the pipelines.\n69\t- Delete the features that read a stage's output. These are scope splits,\n70\t  discover questions, the acceptance tests, UAT sign-off, learn, escapes,\n71\t  and the CI and deploy watches.\n72\t- A migration moves stored jobs and workflows to steps.\n73\t- DECISIONS.md marks each replaced decision, and the README says what\n74\t  Bastion is now.\n75\t- Exit: no `Stage` in the code but the five kinds, and every CI step passes.\n76\t\n77\t## Open questions\n78\t\n79\t- **What learn and escapes become.** Both read a landed job's history. They"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"12f5a07d-bcc5-491f-8fd9-e4afee845ab9","timestamp":"2026-11-11T18:45:54.910Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nNote: /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/PLAN.md changed on disk since you last read it. That's usually deliberate, so take it as the current state rather than reverting it; if the change looks wrong, say so rather than undoing it yourself — otherwise no need to call it out. Here are the relevant changes (shown with line numbers):\n1\t# Bastion plan: a workflow is its steps\n2\t\n3\t<!-- voice-register: informational -->\n4\t<!-- voice-english: ste -->\n5\t\n6\tA job runs its workflow's steps in order, and nothing else. A step is one of\n7\tfive kinds, and each kind has one text to edit. Bastion seeds the common\n8\tworkflows with that text filled in, and the owner can change all of it. The\n9\tstage concept goes away: no step has behavior that its text does not say.\n10\tThe cost is the safety the built-in stages gave. Landing, pull requests,\n11\tmerges, quality gates and acceptance tests become text the owner can read\n12\tand change, not code Bastion enforces.\n13\t\n...\n45\t  land stage accepts such a workflow.\n46\t- The workspace gets its finish setting: merge, or leave the branch.\n47\t- Exit, live: a workflow of a planning prompt, a building prompt, a test\n48\t  shell step and a network shell step that opens a pull request on\n49\t  `ianparo/bastion-demo`.\n50\t\n51\t### S2. The seeded workflows are steps\n52\t\n53\t- Each seeded workflow becomes the five kinds, with its text filled in.\n54\t- The plan and build prompts start from today's briefs, and review is a\n55\t  prompt. The pull request, the CI wait and the merge are `gh` shell scripts.\n56\t- \"Runs as\" lists only the five kinds.\n57\t- Exit, live: the seeded short workflow lands a change on `demo-local`, and\n58\t  the seeded pull request workflow opens and merges a pull request.\n59\t\n60\t### S3. Everything names steps\n61\t\n62\t- Governance per step (D42), dials per step only, notices and the job\n63\t  page by step id, evals by step.\n64\t- Exit, live: a read-only step's write is refused and noted in the audit.\n65\t\n66\t### S4. The stage code goes\n67\t\n68\t- Delete the built-in stages and the pipelines.\n69\t- Delete the features that read a stage's output. These are scope splits,\n70\t  discover questions, the acceptance tests, UAT sign-off, learn, escapes,\n71\t  and the CI and deploy watches.\n72\t- A migration moves stored jobs and workflows to steps.\n73\t- DECISIONS.md marks each replaced decision, and the README says what\n74\t  Bastion is now.\n75\t- Exit: no `Stage` in the code but the five kinds, and every CI step passes.\n76\t\n77\t## Open questions\n78\t\n79\t- **What learn and escapes become.** Both read a landed job's history. They\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"system","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
