{"parentUuid":"a41518c6-b393-437d-96fb-ad8bd656a573","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwqwK5LShFZWygRCQY5W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm starting Hetzner and DigitalOcean hosting-plan subagents in the background while I continue S4, each to first check whether the cloud VPS types expose `/dev/kvm`, which Bastion's sandbox VMs need.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1987,"cache_read_input_tokens":908227,"output_tokens":3582,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":497},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1987,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3582,"cache_read_input_tokens":908227,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1987,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1987},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"5c7d2d7f-5e59-485e-82f4-6d68f3410599","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgwqwJUcpyALDzjre98kP","type":"assistant","uuid":"c2b901cb-f8ab-459a-a50a-8ef94e090dba","timestamp":"2026-11-11T21:23:48.939Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"c2b901cb-f8ab-459a-a50a-8ef94e090dba","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwqwK5LShFZWygRCQY5W","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_015K4rwwwJXv1wXDWn5R1S7F","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Hetzner hosting plan for bastion-server","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Write a plan for installing and running `bastion-server` on a Hetzner machine with Ubuntu, reachable remotely over TLS, so the Bastion desktop app on a Mac can attach to it as a remote server. The owner has no Hetzner account yet, so the plan starts from account creation and machine choice.\n\nContext you must read first, in /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion (a Rust workspace):\n- README.md, VISION.md, DECISIONS.md (especially D30, D31: remote servers, people, tokens, TLS).\n- scripts/install.sh (it supports `--service` to run the server as a systemd user service on Linux, `--version`, `--prefix`).\n- crates/bastion-server/src/tls.rs and how the server takes a TLS certificate (grep for \"tls\", \"cert\", \"--tls\" in crates/bastion-server/src and crates/bastion-cli). Find the exact flags or settings, and the port (the local default is 49190).\n- worker-compose.yaml: the iii stack the server starts (iii engine 0.25.0, its workers, the sandbox worker). Find what the sandbox needs.\n- crates/bastion-server/src/doctor.rs: what Doctor checks before the stack starts (it refuses a machine without nested virtualization, /dev/kvm).\n- How a remote server issues a person a token (grep \"token\", \"people\", \"issue\").\n\nThe deciding constraint: Bastion's agent work runs in sandbox micro-VMs that need hardware virtualization (/dev/kvm) on Linux. Research, with web search, which Hetzner products expose /dev/kvm: Hetzner Cloud VPS (CX, CPX, CAX, CCX lines) generally do not offer nested virtualization; Hetzner dedicated/root servers (for example the AX or EX lines, or the Server Auction) give bare metal with KVM. Confirm what is true now and cite the source URLs. Recommend the cheapest product that works, and say plainly if a Cloud VPS cannot run the sandbox.\n\nThe plan must cover, as numbered steps with exact commands:\n1. Account, project, SSH key, and the machine to order (product, location, rough monthly price, with source).\n2. Ubuntu version and first hardening: a non-root user, SSH keys only, unattended upgrades.\n3. Checking /dev/kvm and virtualization before anything else.\n4. Installing `iii` 0.25.0 and any other dependency the stack needs (find them in the repo: docker or containers? git? gh? say exactly what and why).\n5. Installing bastion-server with scripts/install.sh `--service` (the curl install command the README gives), and enabling lingering for the systemd user service.\n6. A DNS name for the server, and a TLS certificate: Let's Encrypt with certbot (or a reverse proxy such as Caddy if the server's own TLS cannot take a renewing certificate; decide from the code), and renewal.\n7. Firewall: Hetzner's firewall and ufw. Open only SSH and the server's HTTPS port; say which ports the iii engine and workers use, and that they must stay closed to the internet.\n8. Giving the owner a token for the remote server, and adding the server in the desktop app's Settings.\n9. Model provider keys and GH_TOKEN go into the server's vault from the app, never in files.\n10. Backups of the server's data folder, and updating with `bastion update`.\n11. A short \"what can go wrong\" list with the fix for each.\n\nRules for the document:\n- Write it to /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/docs/hosting/hetzner.md. Create the folder. Do not commit, and do not touch any other file.\n- Plain, simplified technical English: one idea per sentence, active voice, imperative steps, no em dashes, sentence-case headings, American spelling.\n- Never invent a flag, a port, or a price. If the code or a source does not settle a fact, write it as an open question.\n- Mark each fact from the web with its source URL.\n\nReply with a short summary: the machine you recommend and why, the ports, how TLS works for this server, and the open questions.","run_in_background":"true"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1987,"cache_read_input_tokens":908227,"output_tokens":3582,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":497},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1987,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3582,"cache_read_input_tokens":908227,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1987,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1987},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_015K4rwwwJXv1wXDWn5R1S7F":{"description":"Hetzner hosting plan for bastion-server","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"Write a plan for installing and running `bastion-server` on a Hetzner machine with Ubuntu, reachable remotely over TLS, so the Bastion desktop app on a Mac can attach to it as a remote server. The owner has no Hetzner account yet, so the plan starts from account creation and machine choice.\n\nContext you must read first, in /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion (a Rust workspace):\n- README.md, VISION.md, DECISIONS.md (especially D30, D31: remote servers, people, tokens, TLS).\n- scripts/install.sh (it supports `--service` to run the server as a systemd user service on Linux, `--version`, `--prefix`).\n- crates/bastion-server/src/tls.rs and how the server takes a TLS certificate (grep for \"tls\", \"cert\", \"--tls\" in crates/bastion-server/src and crates/bastion-cli). Find the exact flags or settings, and the port (the local default is 49190).\n- worker-c