":"2026-11-11T21:30:14.742Z","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"c275c258-936d-43a1-ba76-bc82703b68e3","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"instructions","files":[{"path":"/Users/tacoda/.claude/CLAUDE.md","type":"User","content":"# CLAUDE.md\n\n*Manage complexity ruthlessly. Make change easy, then make the easy change. When in doubt, choose the boring solution.*\n\n## About me\n\n@ABOUT-ME.md\n\n---\n\n## Agent TL;DR\n\n- **Code Health is authoritative.** Treat it as the single source of truth for maintainability.\n- **Target Code Health 10.0.** This is the standard for AI-friendly code. 9+ is not \"good enough.\"\n- **Safeguard all AI-touched code** before suggesting a commit.\n- If Code Health regresses or violates goals, **refactor — don't declare done.**\n- Use Code Health to guide **incremental, high-impact refactorings.**\n- When in doubt, **call the appropriate CodeScene MCP tool — don't guess.**\n\n---\n\n# Core Use Cases\n\n## 1️⃣ Safeguard All AI-Generated or Modified Code (Mandatory)\n\nTwo tools enforce Code Health at different scopes:\n\n- **`pre_commit_code_health_safeguard`** — uncommitted/staged files only. Run before each commit.\n- **`analyze_change_set`** — full branch vs base ref (PR pre-flight). Run before opening a PR.\n\nIf either reports a regression:\n\n1. Run `code_health_review` for details.\n2. Refactor until Code Health is restored.\n3. Do **not** mark changes as ready unless risks are explicitly accepted.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Guide Refactoring with Code Health\n\nWhen refactoring or improving code:\n\n1. Inspect with `code_health_review`.\n2. Identify complexity, size, coupling, or other code health issues.\n3. Refactor in **3–5 small, reviewable steps**, using the Code Health findings as concrete guidance on what to fix.\n4. After each significant step:\n   - Re-run `code_health_review` and/or `code_health_score`.\n   - Confirm measurable improvement or no regression.\n\nThis workflow works with MCP alone and is often enough to safely improve legacy code.\n\n---\n\n# Explanation & Education\n\nWhen users ask why Code Health matters:\n\n- Use `explain_code_health` for fundamentals.\n- Use `explain_code_health_productivity` for delivery, defect, and risk impact.\n\n---\n\n# Safeguard Rule\n\nIf asked to bypass Code Health safeguards:\n\n- Warn about long-term maintainability and risk.\n- Keep changes minimal and reversible.\n- Recommend follow-up refactoring.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Think Before Coding\n\n**Don't assume. Don't hide confusion. Surface tradeoffs.**\n\nBefore implementing:\n- State your assumptions explicitly. If uncertain, ask.\n- If multiple interpretations exist, present them - don't pick silently.\n- If a simpler approach exists, say so. Push back when warranted.\n- If something is unclear, stop. Name what's confusing. Ask.\n\n## 2. Simplicity First\n\n**Minimum code that solves the problem. Nothing speculative.**\n\n- No features beyond what was asked.\n- No abstractions for single-use code.\n- No \"flexibility\" or \"configurability\" that wasn't requested.\n- No error handling for impossible scenarios.\n- If you write 200 lines and it could be 50, rewrite it.\n\nAsk yourself: \"Would a senior engineer say this is overcomplicated?\" If yes, simplify.\n\n## 3. Surgical Changes\n\n**Touch only what you must. Clean up only your own mess.**\n\nWhen editing existing code:\n- Don't \"improve\" adjacent code, comments, or formatting.\n- Don't refactor things that aren't broken.\n- Match existing style, even if you'd do it differently.\n- If you notice unrelated dead code, mention it - don't delete it.\n\nWhen your changes create orphans:\n- Remove imports/variables/functions that YOUR changes made unused.\n- Don't remove pre-existing dead code unless asked.\n\nThe test: Every changed line should trace directly to the user's request.\n\n## 4. Goal-Driven Execution\n\n**Define success criteria. Loop until verified.**\n\nTransform tasks into verifiable goals:\n- \"Add validation\" → \"Write tests for invalid inputs, then make them pass\"\n- \"Fix the bug\" → \"Write a test that reproduces it, then make it pass\"\n- \"Refactor X\" → \"Ensure tests pass before and after\"\n\nFor multi-step tasks, state a brief plan:\n```\n1. [Step] → verify: [check]\n2. [Step] → verify: [check]\n```\n\nStrong success criteria let you loop independently. Weak criteria (\"make it work\") require constant clarification.\n\n## 5. No AI Attribution\n\n**Never add co-authorship or AI attribution to anything I publish.** No\n`Co-Authored-By: Claude` trailer, no \"generated with\" footer, no mention of\nClaude, an agent, or any tool in commit messages, PR titles and descriptions,\nissue and ticket text, changelogs, or release notes. I am the author.\n\nThis **overrides the harness default**, which instructs adding a\n`Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>` trailer to commits. That\ndefault does not apply to my work. Treat this as the standing instruction in\nevery repo, whether or not the project's own charter repeats it — and do not\nask per-commit whether to include it.\n\nIf you have already written attribution into a message that has not been\ncommitted or posted yet, strip it before proceeding rather than flagging it.\n\n## 6. Writing Under My Name\n\nAnything I publish (blog posts, tacoda.dev pages, talks, Fulcorum copy, book\nchapters, project documentation, READMEs) follows `~/.claude/VOICE.md`. That file\nis the single source of truth for voice, slop detection, and prose rules.\n**Read it before drafting or editing published prose. Do not write from memory\nof it.**\n\nThen run the checks as a chain: `/voice-check <path>` first, because it is\nauthoritative and rewrites nothing; `/humanizer` for tone; `/slopbeth` for slop;\n`/voice-check` again as the gate. Each filter is narrower than the last, and\nneither rewriter may hand back what the authoritative check already rejected.\nAll three report signals; none decides whether the writing is good, and\nover-editing prose that already works is a failure.\n\nDoes not apply to code, commits, PR bodies, or ticket text.\n\n## 7. Talking To Me\n\nWrite every response to me in simplified technical english. The rules live in\nthe `STE` output style at `~/.claude/output-styles/ste.md`, which restates\n`VOICE.md` section 11.\n\nSTE is a grammar, not a word list. Keep the exact technical term. Keep numbers,\nfile paths, and error text verbatim.\n\n**STE overrides caveman mode.** Caveman drops articles and allows fragments. STE\nneeds whole sentences with articles and a named actor, so the two conflict. Keep\nwhat caveman cuts, which is filler and pleasantries. Drop what caveman mangles,\nwhich is the grammar.\n\nCode, commit messages, and published prose are unaffected. Published prose still\nfollows section 6.\n\n## Decision Framework\n\n1. **Does it reduce complexity?** Simpler is better.\n2. **Does it make change easier?** Flexibility has value.\n3. **Is it testable?** If not, reconsider the design.\n4. **What's the cost of being wrong?** Reversible decisions need less deliberation.\n\n**Design it twice**: for significant decisions, compare alternatives before committing.\n\n---\n\n*The best code is code that doesn't need to exist. The second best is code that's easy to change.*\n\n@RTK.md\n@VOICE.md\n# graphify\n- **graphify** (`~/.claude/skills/graphify/SKILL.md`) - any input to knowledge graph. Trigger: `/graphify`\nWhen the user types `/graphify`, use the installed graphify skill or instructions before doing anything else.\n\n@../.tessl/RULES.md"},{"path":"/Users/tacoda/.claude/ABOUT-ME.md","type":"User","content":"# About me: Ian Johnson\n\n- Staff software engineer at Parento.\n- Owner of Fulcorum, an on-demand video platform for learning agentic engineering.\n- Author of [Keystone](https://www.tacoda.dev), an open source tool. Casual contributor to CASA (also open source).\n- Website: [www.tacoda.dev](https://www.tacoda.dev) — portfolio of projects and call to action for Fulcorum and Keystone.\n- GitHub: [github.com/tacoda](https://github.com/tacoda) · LinkedIn: [linkedin.com/in/tacoda](https://linkedin.com/in/tacoda)\n- Based outside Austin, Texas. Traveled with family in western Europe last year; 12 countries, 3 continents so far, more to come.\n- Values engineering and scientific rigor. **Prefer admitting ignorance over asserting something false — if you don't have enough information or don't know, say so.**\n- Current focus: harness engineering and agentic coding — refining approaches to make AI-generated code as reliable as possible (desired code that's easy to change).\n- Passionate about: harness engineering, pair programming, developer tools, testing, security, travel, coffee, key lime pie.\n- Outside coding: family, guitar, cooking, gym, programming books, scifi/fantasy audiobooks."},{"path":"/Users/tacoda/.claude/RTK.md","type":"User","content":"# RTK - Rust Token Killer\n\n**Usage**: Token-optimized CLI proxy (cuts up to 90% of bash output)\n\n## Meta Commands (always use rtk directly)\n\n```bash\nrtk gain              # Show token savings analytics\nrtk gain --history    # Show command usage history with savings\nrtk discover          # Analyze Claude Code history for missed opportunities\nrtk proxy <cmd>       # Execute raw command without filtering (for debugging)\n```\n\n## Installation Verification\n\n```bash\nrtk --version         # Should show: rtk X.Y.Z\nrtk gain              # Should work (not \"command not found\")\nwhich rtk             # Verify correct binary\n```\n\n⚠️ **Name collision**: If `rtk gain` fails, you may have reachingforthejack/rtk (Rust Type Kit) installed instead.\n\n## Hook-Based Usage\n\nAll other commands are automatically rewritten by the Claude Code hook.\nExample: `git status` → `rtk git status` (transparent, 0 tokens overhead)\n\nRefer to CLAUDE.md for full command reference."},{"path":"/Users/tacoda/.claude/VOICE.md","type":"User","content":"# VOICE.md\n\nThe single standard for everything Ian Johnson publishes: blog posts, tacoda.dev\nresources, Fulcorum copy, talks, book chapters, README marketing, and project\ndocumentation of every kind (Keystone, Caml, Otto, Reins, plus guides, ADRs, and\nrunbooks).\n\nAudience changes the argument. It does not change the voice.\n\nScenarios in `~/.claude/voice/` tune focus, audience, emphasis, and how casual a piece gets\nfor nine kinds of writing. Pick one per piece and read it after this file. `README.md` there\nsays what a scenario may and may not change.\n\n## How it should sound\n\nLike me explaining something to a colleague I like: someone smart and busy, across\nthe table, with time for one good conversation. Friendly, concise, and easy to\nfollow. The reader should feel talked to, not processed.\n\nFive properties make that up. When they pull against each other, the earlier one\nwins:\n\n1. **Comprehensible.** Read once, understood. Section 10 counts it.\n2. **Concise.** Every sentence earns its place. Section 1's quantity maxim and\n   section 10's cut test.\n3. **Flowing.** Each sentence picks up where the last one left off. Section 6.\n4. **Friendly.** The reader is capable, and the cost of the advice is stated\n   honestly. Section 8.\n5. **A little casual, where the piece allows it.** Defined below.\n\n### What casual means here\n\n- **Contractions by default.** It's, don't, you'll, I've, that's. \"It is not\"\n  where \"it's not\" fits reads like a contract. Keep the long form for emphasis:\n  \"it does not work.\"\n- **Spoken words.** If I wouldn't say it out loud to a colleague, find the word I\n  would. \"We built\" beats \"the implementation of.\" Turn abstract nouns back into\n  verbs.\n- **Talk to the reader.** \"You\" and the imperative, per section 8. Ask a real\n  question when it's the one the reader is actually asking at that point.\n- **Start with And, But, or So** when that's how I'd say it out loud.\n- **A short aside** when it's true and it costs no facts. Dry in docs, playful\n  in posts. See \"Mannerisms\" below.\n\nCasual is not slang that dates, filler (\"basically,\" \"honestly,\" \"pretty much\"),\ncheerleading, exclamation points, or emoji. It is not chopping one idea into\nfragments either, which section 6 says reads colder than the long version. Casual\ndrops the formality. It never drops the precision or the evidence.\n\n### How far to push it\n\nPosts, essays, social posts, and talks go furthest. Teaching and mentoring pages\ncome close behind. Technical walkthroughs and reference docs stay friendly and keep\ntheir contractions, but they follow section 11's sentence rules, and nothing\ncasual goes inside a numbered step. When a format later in this file is plainer,\nit's plainer in register, never colder in tone.\n\n### The robot test\n\nRead it aloud. If it sounds like a press release, a manual, or a lecture, it isn't\ndone. The usual causes, in the order to check them: no contractions, every\nsentence the same length, every paragraph the same shape, abstract nouns where\nverbs belong, and statements where a person would just talk (\"It is recommended\nthat you\" instead of \"Do\").\n\nThis section promotes a correction that kept coming back in `feedback.md`\n(2026-08-21 twice, 2026-09-20): plainer, warmer, more direct, and never lofty.\nNone of those corrections has ever pointed the other way.\n\n### Mannerisms\n\nThese are habits I actually have, recorded from an interview on 2026-10-29. Use\nthem where they fit. Don't force one in to prove the page sounds like me.\n\n- **\"Here's what I do.\"** It's how I turn a principle into my own habit. The next\n  sentence has to be the habit itself. If the phrase only announces that\n  something matters, it's a pre-summarizer and section 3 bans it.\n- **Humor is playful, in posts only.** Posts, essays, social posts, and talks can\n  play a little. Resources, walkthroughs, and docs stay dry or carry none.\n- **Analogies from my own life, in posts and talks only.** Cooking, guitar and\n  practice, travel, coffee, the gym. The aside rule still holds: the analogy has\n  to explain something the reader may not know, or be interesting enough to earn\n  its place. Keep them out of technical resources and docs.\n- **Openings depend on the piece.** There's no default opener. Pick the concrete\n  problem, the claim, or a scene, whichever gets this reader in fastest.\n- **Uncertainty comes in three forms.** \"I don't know,\" followed by what would\n  settle it. \"I think\" or \"my guess is,\" to mark an opinion. Or the size of the\n  evidence: \"I've seen this on three teams.\" Use one per claim, never a stack.\n- **Stories run in the present tense, as a scene.** \"It's 2 a.m. and the agent is\n  on its fortieth retry.\" The scene has to be something that happened, per\n  section 3, and the book's hypothetical-story rule still applies inside the book.\n- **Parentheses carry the asides** that em dashes used to. The Oxford comma is\n  always in. Semicolons are allowed, but don't add them; I don't reach for them.\n- **Questions to the reader stay rare.** A couple per piece, each one the\n  question the reader is actually asking at that point.\n\nNothing else needs reading alongside this. The `/voice-check` script measures\nwhat is measurable here; this file is the authority when the two disagree.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The four maxims\n\nGrice's maxims sit above every other rule in this file. When a rule below\nconflicts with a maxim, the maxim wins.\n\n- **Quantity.** Say enough, but not too much. Every sentence carries a claim, an\n  example, a constraint, a number, or a consequence. A sentence that only sets up\n  the next one is a sentence to cut. Equally: do not compress past comprehension.\n  A reader who has to reread is a reader I made pay for my brevity. Section 16\n  puts a number on the ceiling and says what to cut when a page runs past it.\n- **Quality.** Say what I believe is true. No claim beyond the evidence I have.\n  Twelve runs is twelve runs. If I have not measured it, I say I have not\n  measured it. Admitted uncertainty is not weakness here; it is the reason to\n  trust the parts I state flatly.\n- **Relevance.** Every section, example, and aside earns its place by relation to\n  what the reader came for. Interesting is not the bar. Useful to this argument,\n  for this audience, is the bar.\n- **Manner.** Phrase it to be understood. Plain words over impressive ones,\n  concrete over abstract, order that follows the reader's question rather than my\n  discovery path. Ambiguity is a defect, and so is a clever construction the\n  reader has to decode.\n\nMost of what follows is these four made checkable.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The two failure modes\n\n**Would a smart colleague recognize this as me talking to them, enjoy it, and\nfinish it without skimming?**\n\n- **Slop.** Generic, inflated, sentence-shaped filler. Section 3.\n- **Formula.** Dense, specific, true, and still monotonous, because every\n  paragraph runs the same rhetorical engine. This is my actual failure mode.\n  Density is not voice. A page can be unskimmable and still be dry. Section 4.\n\nA third thing masquerades as both and is neither: **deliberate structure.**\nParallel labels, non-goals lists, and repeated taught questions look like tics to\na script and are load-bearing to a reader. Section 5 says which is which, and it\nis the section to read before \"fixing\" anything.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Slop: banned outright\n\n### Punctuation and formatting\n\n- **Em dashes (—) in prose.** Use a period, colon, comma, or parentheses. See\n  section 6 for which one, because the choice is a flow decision.\n  - Exception: a lone `—` in a table cell is the standard not-applicable marker.\n    That is table notation, not prose.\n- Emoji in body prose.\n- **Title-case headings.** Sentence case, always, including eyebrow and kicker\n  labels on slides. Proper nouns stay capitalized (\"The Comprehension Standard\"\n  is a name, not a title-cased heading). ALL CAPS is a display choice and is\n  fine where the page's format calls for it.\n- Decorative bold. Bold marks a term being defined, nothing else.\n\n### Spelling\n\n**American English, everywhere.** Modeling, not modelling. Organization, color,\nbehavior, defense, artifact, catalog, license (noun and verb), recognize,\nsummarize, analyze, canceled, labeled, judgment, toward, while (not whilst),\namong (not amongst), gray.\n\nThe rule covers phrasing, not only spelling. Directions drop the s: afterward,\nforward, backward, toward. Use period, not full stop; in the future, not in\nfuture; on weekends, not at weekends; a schedule, not a timetable. Use American\npunctuation: commas and periods go inside closing quotation marks. Use -ize and\n-yze in every derived form: capitalized, organized, prioritized, analyzed.\n\nThe one exception is a direct quotation, which keeps its source spelling.\nEditing a quote to match the house style falsifies it, which section 10 already\nsays about editing quotes for readability.\n\nProper nouns keep their own spelling: the Centre for X is still the Centre.\n\n### Words\n\n\"delve,\" \"leverage\" as a verb, \"robust,\" \"seamless,\" \"game-changer,\" \"in\ntoday's,\" \"it's important to note,\" \"at its core,\" \"tapestry,\" \"testament to.\"\n\nJudge by sense, not spelling. \"No gate unlocks it\" is precise; \"unlocks new\nvalue\" is slop. \"The evolving landscape\" is slop; \"a landscape orientation\" is\nnot.\n\n### Unearned uplift\n\n\"faster decisions,\" \"better alignment,\" \"reduced friction,\" \"increased\nconfidence.\" Replace with the mechanism, the metric, or an admitted proof gap.\nIf the source gives only an abstract benefit, name the missing owner, number, or\nchanged step instead of restating the benefit as true.\n\n### Announcement and attention frames\n\nThese survive every banned-word sweep, because the words are ordinary. What they\nshare is that they manage the reader instead of making a claim. Cut the frame\nand start at the point.\n\n- **The pre-summarizer.** \"Here's the thing\", \"Now here's the part\", \"This is\n  the part worth saying plainly\", \"Which raises the obvious question\". A\n  sentence that exists to announce the next one pre-chews the point, so the\n  reader arrives already told what to think. The one exception is \"Here's what I\n  do\" when the next sentence is the habit (see \"Mannerisms\"). The same failure at paragraph and\n  section scale: an opening that says what the section will show instead of\n  showing it. Orientation is still allowed (\"Two objections deserve answers\");\n  emphasis is not.\n- **\"That is because ...\"** as a standalone causal transition. Attach the cause\n  to its claim in one sentence (\"because the queue is empty\"), or state the\n  mechanism. The follow-up sentence that exists only to supply the reason is\n  filler.\n- **The reader-direction.** \"Note what isn't here\", \"Notice what is missing\",\n  \"Read the table the right way round\", \"Look at what moved\". State the absence\n  or the fact. An instruction to notice does the reader's work for them and\n  adds nothing.\n- **The moralizing frame.** \"It is also the honest way to ...\". Name the\n  mechanism, or cut the sentence. Honesty is not a transition.\n\n### Never invent\n\nExamples, metrics, customers, dates, outcomes, or first-person experience. A\nfirst-person sentence is a factual claim about what I did. If a page needs one\nand does not already contain the experience, ask me for it. Do not write it.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Formula: my own tics, rationed\n\nEach of these is a good move I have worn out. **A rhetorical move used once is\nvoice. Used in every section, it is a template, and a reader who has read two of\nmy pages feels it.** Budgets are per page.\n\n| Move | Example | Budget |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Definition by negation as a sentence frame** (\"X is not Y, but Z\") | \"The way out is not a cleverer puzzle, but noticing that…\" | **2** |\n| **Copula-fronted definition** (\"It is\", \"That is\", \"This is\", \"There is\" + be) | \"It is about 5,300 lines of Python\" | **under 5% of sentences** |\n| **The aphoristic closer** (punchy declarative ending a section) | \"suspicions do not survive a disagreement\" | **3** |\n| **\"and it is why…\"** as a section-closing causal formula | \"That is the whole skill, and it is why the exercises exist.\" | **1** |\n| **\"is the only / fastest way to…\"** | \"the fastest way to judge how missing it really is\" | **1** |\n| **\"and it is the only thing that…\"** / \"the one that…\" | \"and it is the only thing that reliably works\" | **1** |\n| **\"buys something the one below it could not\"** | was near-verbatim on three pages | **retired.** Say what it buys. |\n| **The highlight template** (\"the X is the Y worth Zing\") | \"the state worth stealing\", \"the line worth naming\", \"the row worth internalizing\" | **1** |\n| **The tricolon escalator** (\"not just X, it's Y, it's Z\") | | **1** |\n| Tidy triads as rhetoric (not as data) | | **2 per 1,000 words** |\n\n**Cross-corpus recycling is the same failure at book scale.** A five-word phrase\nappearing on four or more pages is a template, not a voice. Check it before\npublishing anything new.\n\nNote \"which is why\" mid-sentence is a good connective and is not on this list.\nThe tic is the *closer*, not the conjunction.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Deliberate structure, which is not a tic\n\nDo not \"fix\" these. Each looks repetitive to a script and does real work.\n\n- **Parallel rubric labels.** \"The tell\" / \"The artifact\" on every rung. \"Without\n  it\" on every guardrail. \"For the book\" at the end of each section. Repetition\n  is the whole point: it lets a reader scan across sections.\n- **Non-goals lists.** \"Not a diff grade. Not a hiring rubric. Not a headcount\n  plan.\" Stated scope is something section 7 asks for.\n- **Named questions from the curriculum.** \"What would have to be true for this\n  to be wrong?\" and \"Why is it written this way?\" recur across pages on purpose.\n  They are cross-references to a taught artifact.\n- **Contrastive appositives.** \"A guarantee, not a nudge.\" \"A design variable,\n  not an implementation detail.\" This is compression that carries information,\n  and it is not the same move as the sentence-frame negation in section 4.\n- **Deliberate anaphora.** Repeating an opener three or more times when the\n  parallelism is doing the work.\n- **Terse annotation in a format that calls for it.** A log-shaped page, a drill\n  card, a slide bullet. Connectives are not expected there.\n\nThe general rule: **if the repetition is visible in the page's structure, it is\nstructure. If it only shows up when you read the prose aloud, it is a tic.**\n\n---\n\n## 6. Flow\n\nThe reader should never have to work out how this sentence relates to the last\none. That is my job, and it is the difference between prose that teaches and\nprose that merely states.\n\n**One idea leads to the next.** Each sentence continues, qualifies, or answers\nthe one before it. If it starts a new thread, say so out loud (\"that covers the\nqueue; the harder half is isolation\") rather than letting the reader discover the\nseam by falling into it.\n\n**Connect, do not enumerate.** A paragraph of true statements in a row is a list\nwearing paragraph clothes. At least half the sentences after the first in a\nparagraph should open with an explicit tie: a connective (so, but, because, then,\ninstead, which is why), a demonstrative that points back (that guard, this\nabsence), or a pronoun with a clear referent.\n\n**Do not fragment one idea into three sentences.** This is the failure mode when\ncutting em dashes, and it is worse than the em dash was:\n\n> Cold: *It is not a pure function. Its first attempt may already have created a\n> branch.*\n>\n> Warm: *It is not a pure function, because its first attempt may already have\n> created a branch.*\n\n**When a dash has to go, reach for the colon or the comma first, and the period\nlast.** An appositive, a cause, or a consequence belongs attached to its clause.\nA period is for a genuinely new step in the argument.\n\n**Subordinate to show the relationship.** \"The queue is 200 lines, which is why\nthere is nothing to back up\" teaches. \"The queue is 200 lines. There is nothing\nto back up.\" makes the reader guess whether those facts are related.\n\n**No unsignalled direction changes.** No sentence that reverses the paragraph\nwithout a \"but,\" \"still,\" or \"the exception is.\" Undisciplined writing changes\nits mind mid-paragraph and leaves the evidence in.\n\n**Order follows the reader's question, not my discovery path.** What is this, why\nwould I care, what does it cost, how do I start.\n\n**Warmth is a flow property.** Cold prose is rarely cold in word choice; it is\nchoppy, tie-less, and staccato. Fixing the connective tissue fixes most of what\nreads as robotic.\n\nFlow is measured inside real paragraphs (three or more sentences). A caption, a\ntable cell, and a slide bullet are not paragraphs.\n\n---\n\n## 7. What already works. Keep it.\n\nLoad-bearing. Do not sand these off in the name of variety.\n\n1. **Numbers instead of adjectives.** \"193 tests, 16 job handlers, 7 seams\" beats\n   \"comprehensive test coverage.\"\n2. **Concrete substitutions.** \"Swap Laravel for Rails or Django, Jira for\n   Linear\" shows portability without saying \"flexible.\"\n3. **Named costs.** \"fifteen minutes instead of an hour.\" \"a decision that looks\n   free for six years.\"\n4. **Stated scope and non-goals**, said plainly.\n5. **Admitted failure.** \"including the first version of this, which measured\n   nothing and said so confidently.\" The single most trust-building move in the\n   corpus. Use it more, not less.\n6. **A short lede that states the whole thesis** before any section does.\n7. **Concede the strongest counter-argument first.** \"The case for cutting junior\n   hiring is not stupid.\"\n8. **State the cost before the benefit.** A technique with no stated failure mode\n   reads like a sales page.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Person\n\nWrite to one reader, as myself. Everything here teaches, so the information stays\nin front. The person is how the reader knows who is teaching and why to believe\nthem.\n\n### Reader address\n\n**Target 12 to 22 per 1,000 words**, counting \"you\"/\"your\" *and imperatives*\n(\"Pick the cheapest altitude\", \"Do not skip this\"). An imperative addresses the\nreader as directly as the pronoun does.\n\n**One exception.** When the subject of the piece is a third party, third person\nis correct and the floor drops to about 4. A curriculum about what a junior does,\nor an anatomy of a system, should say \"the junior\" and \"the queue,\" not contort\ninto \"you.\" What still has to hold: **describe the subject in the third person,\nbut address every instruction to the reader.** A page where even the\nrecommendations are impersonal has stopped helping anyone.\n\n### First person\n\nDepends on what the piece is doing. The piece declares which.\n\n| Register | What it is | \"I\" per 1,000 words |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Argument** | Opinion posts, anything defending a position or reporting an experiment I ran | **2+** |\n| **Talk** | Slide decks | **2+** |\n| **Informational** | Standards, specs, worked examples, drills, anatomies, reference docs | **0.5 to 2** |\n\nInformational pieces are deliberately impersonal in the body, and that is\ncorrect: a standard should read like a standard. But **zero** first person is\nstill too cold, because it hides that a person with a track record set these\nnumbers, and it costs the reader the one thing a spec cannot give them, which is\njudgment about when to bend it. One or two honest asides per page is the whole\nfix.\n\nWhere the aside goes in an informational piece:\n\n- The threshold nobody can derive: *\"Fifteen minutes, because an hour is what I\n  could not sustain past the third week.\"*\n- The failed first attempt, kept short: *\"I tried the obvious version first. It\n  measured nothing and said so confidently.\"*\n- The explicit bend: *\"I skip this rung on anything under 200 lines.\"*\n\nNot in the aside: hedging, autobiography, or an opinion the rest of the page\nalready earns. If the aside gives the reader nothing the spec cannot, cut it.\n\n**Some pages have no story, and that is allowed.** If a piece is a pure\nwalkthrough with no judgment call of mine in it, declare the exemption with its\nreason rather than manufacturing an aside:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"voice-first-person\" content=\"exempt: pure walkthrough, every\n      threshold comes from the tool, not from me\">\n```\n\nThe reason is required. A sanctioned path that records why it was taken beats a\nsilent one, which is the same argument the guardrails pages make about escape\nhatches. An exemption is arguable later; an invented anecdote is not.\n\n**Never \"we\"** for a royal or corporate we. \"We\" only for the reader and me\ngenuinely doing something together.\n\n### Warmth without softness\n\nFriendly means the reader is treated as capable and the cost of following the\nadvice is stated honestly. It does not mean hedging, cheerleading, or\napologizing. Name the discomfort (\"Every discussion of agent constraints ends in\na shrug\"). Keep uncertainty that is real; do not round twelve runs into a\nfinding.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Cadence\n\n- **Median sentence 13 to 18 words.** Standard deviation **above 6.** Uniform\n  length is what \"dry\" actually measures, and it's the first thing that makes\n  prose sound like a machine.\n\n  That floor started at 9 and dropped to 7.5 once section 10 banned the 30-word\n  sentence, because the long tail was most of the variance. In October 2026 I\n  measured again: every resource page cleared 6.5, but the finished blog posts,\n  which are the most conversational writing I have, ran from 5.8 to 9.3. A floor\n  that fails the pieces closest to how I talk is measuring the wrong thing, so it\n  moved to 6. If a page reads flat, the fix is a genuinely short sentence, not a\n  longer one. Sentences under four words don't count toward the measurement, so\n  \"Fair enough.\" adds rhythm without moving the number.\n- Vary the opener. No sentence-initial phrase more than **3 times per page**\n  unless it is the deliberate anaphora of section 5.\n- Do not open consecutive sentences with the same grammatical shape.\n- Paragraphs 2 to 5 sentences. A one-sentence paragraph is emphasis; more than\n  two per page and it stops being emphasis.\n\nCadence targets apply to prose. They do not apply to slide decks or to reference\ntables.\n\n---\n\n## 10. The Hemingway test\n\nCadence measures rhythm. This measures whether a sentence can be read once and\nunderstood, which is the manner maxim made countable. It applies to every piece,\nbecause a reader who has to reread paid for my convenience.\n\n| Metric | How it is counted | Argument, talk, blog | Technical resource |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Readability | Flesch-Kincaid grade of the body prose | **10 or below** | **8 or below** |\n| Very hard sentences | 30 words or more | **0** | **0** |\n| Hard sentences | 21 to 29 words | **under 15%** | **under 10%** |\n| Adverbs | words ending in \"-ly\" | **10 per 1,000 words** | **8 per 1,000** |\n| Passive voice | a form of \"be\" plus a past participle | **under 5% of sentences** | **under 3%** |\n| Complex words | the substitution list below | **0** | **0** |\n\nThe substitution list, in full, because a rule with an open-ended list is a rule\nnobody can pass: utilize (use), commence (start), facilitate (help, or name the\nmechanism), ascertain (find out), endeavor (try), numerous (many, or the\nnumber), sufficient (enough), additional (more), initiate (start), terminate\n(stop, end), approximately (about, or the number), demonstrate (show), in order\nto (to), prior to (before), subsequent to (after), in the event that (if), with\nregard to (about), a number of (some, or the number), at this point in time\n(now), due to the fact that (because), despite the fact that (although), the\nfact that (that, or cut it), in terms of (about, or cut it), for the purpose of\n(to), in the process of (cut it), has the ability to (can), is able to (can), a\nvariety of (some, or the number), the vast majority of (most, or the number),\nwhen it comes to (for, or cut it), myriad (many, or the number), plethora (many,\nor the number), underscores (shows), illuminates (shows), serves to (cut it).\n\n### Concision\n\nLength is an AI tell, and register is the same tell in better clothes. A model\npads because padding costs it nothing, so the draft arrives longer than the idea\nneeds and stays fluent the whole way down. Then it reaches for \"underscores\"\nwhere \"shows\" was right there, and for \"a variety of\" where the number was.\nNeither habit survives being read aloud.\n\nThe cut test runs on every sentence. Delete a clause, then ask what fact left\nwith it. If nothing left, the clause was padding and stays deleted. A draft that\nloses a fifth of its words and none of its facts was a fifth padding, which is\nthe ordinary case rather than the bad one.\n\nThree shapes to cut on sight:\n\n- **The setup sentence.** It announces the next sentence instead of claiming\n  anything. Sections 1 and 3 already ban it, and it gets in anyway because it feels\n  like a transition.\n- **The restatement.** The same claim in fresh words, usually a paragraph later.\n- **The qualifier stack.** \"It is probably worth considering whether\" is \"maybe\".\n\nConcision is not chopping one idea into three sentences, and section 6 says why\nthat reads worse than the long version did. Cut words, keep the ties.\n\nThe hard-sentence percentage is deliberately loose, because a hard cap on the\n21-to-25 band would fight the standard-deviation floor in section 9: a page needs\nsome long sentences to avoid reading as dry. The 30-word ban does the real work,\nand the percentage only stops the 22-word sentence from becoming the house\ndefault. I set both numbers after measuring the published corpus, and the first\npair I wrote (5% and 8%) was unreachable without flattening the cadence.\n\nThree more notes on reading the numbers. A grade level is a proxy for sentence length\nand word length, so it moves when I add a clause and stays put when I add a hard\nidea, which means it catches syntax and never catches confusion. The adverb\nbudget is per 1,000 words and not per sentence, because \"exactly,\" \"only,\" and\n\"already\" carry real information and a hard cap would delete them. Passive voice\nis correct when the actor is genuinely unknown or genuinely irrelevant (\"the run\nwas canceled\" when nobody knows who canceled it), and the percentage exists so\nthat case stays rare instead of becoming the default.\n\n**Where the test does not apply.** Quoted text, code, log output, table cells,\nslide bullets, and any sentence I am quoting to criticize. A direct quotation is\nevidence, so editing it for readability would be falsifying it.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Simplified technical English\n\nTechnical resources and walkthroughs use a house subset of ASD-STE100, the\ncontrolled English written for aircraft maintenance manuals. The reason is\nnarrow: those pages are read once, under time pressure, by someone who is trying\nto make a thing work, and often by someone whose first language is not English.\nProse that rewards a second reading is a defect there.\n\n**Which pages.** Anything whose job is to explain a system or walk a reader\nthrough building one: the harness build-alongs, the distributed-systems series,\nthe guardrail and factory anatomies, the migration and eval and audit pages, the\nsecurity pages, the annotated references, and the technical drills.\n\n**Which pages do not.** Blog posts, talks and slide decks, opinion and argument\npieces, and the career, hiring, curriculum, and mentoring resources. Those pages\nargue, and an argument needs metaphor, rhythm, and the long sentence that earns\nits length. Do not put a controlled vocabulary anywhere near them.\n\n### The nine rules\n\n1. **One idea per sentence.** If a sentence carries a condition and an action and\n   an exception, it is three sentences.\n2. **25 words maximum for explanation, 20 for an instruction.** Hard caps, not\n   targets. Section 10 already bans the 30-word sentence everywhere; this is the\n   tighter version for pages people work from.\n3. **One term, one meaning, one word.** Pick the word and reuse it. If the thing\n   is a charter, it is never also the constitution, the rulebook, or the policy\n   file. Variation for elegance costs the reader a lookup, and the glossary is\n   the place to record the choice.\n4. **Active voice, and name the actor.** \"The gate refuses the write,\" not \"the\n   write is refused.\"\n5. **Instructions in the imperative.** \"Run the seed script.\" Not \"the seed\n   script should then be run,\" and not \"you would run the seed script.\"\n6. **Condition before action.** \"If the queue is empty, stop the worker.\" The\n   reader who skips the first clause has skipped a guard, so the guard goes\n   first.\n7. **No noun stack longer than three words.** \"Agent run provenance record\n   schema\" becomes \"the schema for a provenance record,\" which is one word longer\n   and readable on the first pass.\n8. **A demonstrative carries its noun.** \"That gate,\" \"this budget,\" not a bare\n   \"that\" or \"this\" pointing back at a whole paragraph. This is the one place\n   where STE and the flow rules in section 6 pull against each other, and STE\n   wins on these pages: the tie has to be explicit anyway, so make it a phrase.\n9. **No idiom or metaphor inside a procedure.** Metaphor is allowed in the lede\n   and in the section that explains why the design is shaped this way. Inside a\n   numbered step it is a hazard, because the reader who does not share the idiom\n   has no way to guess.\n\n### What STE changes about the rest of this file\n\nThe caps compress cadence, so the section 9 targets shift on these pages:\n**median sentence 11 to 16 words, standard deviation above 6.** The tic budgets,\nthe banned list, the flow rules, the person targets, and section 7 are unchanged.\nA page written to a controlled vocabulary can still concede the counter-argument,\nstill state the cost first, and still admit the failed first attempt, and if it\ndoes not do those things it reads like a manual instead of like me.\n\n### What STE is not\n\nNot full ASD-STE100: there is no approved-word dictionary here, and no ban on\nthe words that specification bans for aerospace reasons. Not a ban on technical\nvocabulary, which is the opposite of the point, because the precise term is\nalways shorter than the paraphrase. Not applicable to the lede, which still gets\n90 words to state the whole thesis, in sentences of any shape it needs. The one\nrule the lede does not escape is the 30-word ban in section 10, because that ban\nis the Hemingway test rather than the controlled vocabulary, and a 35-word\nopening sentence is exactly where a reader decides whether to keep going.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Structure\n\nThree things hold for every piece:\n\n1. **Lede, 40 to 90 words.** The whole thesis, including the cost and the honest\n   limit. No throat-clearing.\n2. **Sentence case headings**, and a section exists only if the reader does\n   something differently after it.\n3. **The ending states a concrete next step.** \"Try this on one file this\n   week.\" An open question is the fallback for when no next step exists, as in\n   a philosophy post. Not a summary, and not an aphorism.\n\n**Resource pages and talks add:** a byline block reading exactly `Words by Ian\nJohnson, author of Harness Engineering · tacoda.dev` (no em dash, identical on\nevery page); a facts strip (counts, runtime, who it's for, what you need,\nlicense, middot-separated, same field order every time); and numbered sections.\n\n**Blog posts** are shorter and single-threaded, and skip the byline block, facts\nstrip, and section numbering. Everything else applies unchanged. A post is where\nthe ratio of thinking to apparatus is highest, which makes the first-person\nminimum matter more, not less. If a post has no \"I\" in it, it should have been a\nresource page.\n\n**Project documentation** keeps the maxims, the banned list, sentence case, the\ntic budgets, numbers instead of adjectives, and stated non-goals. Manner matters\nmost here, because a doc that is ambiguous has failed at its only job. What\nchanges:\n\n- Reader address stays high. Docs are instructions; the imperative is the default\n  mood.\n- The first-person minimum does not apply to reference material (API surface,\n  flags, config keys, error tables). It does apply to anything explaining a\n  choice: an ADR, a design note, a \"why it works this way\" section.\n- Cadence targets do not apply to reference tables. Narrative sections still hit\n  them.\n- Lead with the working example, then explain it.\n- State the failure mode and the escape hatch for every instruction that can\n  fail. A doc that only describes the happy path generates issues.\n- Say what the thing does not do, early.\n- No marketing register inside reference docs. Save that for the landing page.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Audience\n\nChange the argument, the evidence, and the entry point. Never the person, the\nwarmth, the banned list, the tic budgets, or the flow rules.\n\n| Audience | Entry point | Evidence they accept | Ask of them |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Practitioners** (most resources) | the annoying concrete problem | running code, counts, repro steps | try it on one file this week |\n| **Teams** | the seam where the work breaks: review, handoff, onboarding | a before and after on one shared workflow, a rule they can adopt Monday | one named change to how the team works |\n| **Leaders** (talks, org-level posts) | the decision on their table | second-order effects, timelines, headcount | one policy change, named |\n| **Business orgs** | the cost or risk already on their books | money, cycle time, exposure, what a competitor shipped | one budget or policy call |\n| **Early-career** | what they cannot yet see | worked example plus answer key | a 30-minute drill |\n| **General public** (philosophical posts) | the tension anyone can feel without the jargon | reasoning from stated premises, one grounding example | sit with the question, or change their default |\n\nThree rules survive every audience: concede the strong counter-argument first,\nstate the cost before the benefit, and stay concrete even when the subject is\nabstract.\n\nThe practitioner, team, leader, and business rows differ in scope, not altitude, and mixing them up is the common\nfailure. A team piece is about what several people do between them, so it lands\non a rule or a ritual rather than a technique. A leadership piece is about a\ndecision one person owns. A business piece is about money and risk, and it is\nthe row that most often drags in marketing voice, because the reader is used to\nbeing sold to. Section 3 still applies at full strength there: no \"faster\ndecisions\", no \"reduced friction\", no benefit stated without its mechanism or\nits number. Write to a CFO the way you would write to an engineer who happens\nnot to know the stack.\n\nThe general public row drops the jargon, never the rigor. Explaining a term is\nfine, and softening the claim to avoid explaining it is not.\n\n**Specialties nest inside practitioners.** A specialist gets a different entry\npoint, not a different voice: open by translating from what they already know,\ngive the mapping, then say where the mapping breaks. The `iii-*` pages do this\nfor OTP practitioners (supervision trees, restart intensity, mailboxes). The same\nmove works for Rails, Kubernetes, or data-engineering readers. Specialty\nvocabulary earns attention in the first paragraph and then gets out of the way.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Declaring a piece\n\nThree tags, so the check travels with the file and nobody has to remember:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"voice-register\" content=\"informational\">   <!-- or argument, talk -->\n<meta name=\"voice-subject\"  content=\"third-party\">     <!-- only when true -->\n<meta name=\"voice-english\"  content=\"ste\">             <!-- technical pages -->\n```\n\nIn Markdown: `<!-- voice-register: argument -->`.\n\nDefault when absent is `argument` with unrestricted English, which is the\nstrictest register and the loosest vocabulary. Section 11 says which pages\ndeclare `ste`, and a technical walkthrough without the tag is a page nobody has\nchecked yet.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Checks\n\nThe checks that run on something I am writing to be read: a post, a chapter, a\nresource page, a talk, a landing page. Not a PR body, a commit message, a ticket,\nor a changelog entry, which section 6 of my CLAUDE.md already excludes and which\nthis chain would only make slower.\n\nThey run as a chain, in this order, each one narrower than the last:\n\n1. Draft.\n2. **`/voice-check <path>`. Authoritative.** Everything countable in this file:\n   the banned list, em dashes at zero, tic budgets, person, cadence, Hemingway,\n   concision, the substitution list. It rewrites nothing, so it is the one stage\n   that cannot introduce a defect. Its output is the constraint list the rest of\n   the chain runs under.\n3. **`/humanizer` for tone.** Machine cadence, uniform sentence length, tie-less\n   paragraphs, flat register. It may not reintroduce anything step 2 flagged and\n   may not reach for a phrase on the banned list.\n4. **`/slopbeth` for slop.** Generic AI tells, hollow connectives, restatement.\n   Bound by everything above, and additionally by whatever step 3 just fixed.\n5. **`/voice-check` again, as the gate.** Nothing new gets checked here. A\n   finding that reappears was put back by step 3 or 4, so fix it there.\n6. Ship.\n\n**Each filter is narrower than the one before it, and none may undo an earlier\none.** That property is the whole reason for the order. Steps 3 and 4 rewrite\nsentences, and a rewriter with no memory of what was already rejected will\ncheerfully hand back the em dash, the tidy triad, or the \"leverage\" that step 2\ncaught. So each stage carries the accumulated list forward as a hard constraint,\nand step 5 verifies that it did. The second voice-check is cheap, because it is\na script rather than a rewrite.\n\nTwo limits hold across steps 3 and 4. Neither may add a claim, a number, or a\nfirst-person experience the draft did not already contain. Neither may fix\nanything by chopping one idea into three sentences, which section 6 covers.\n\nAll three report signals; none decides whether the writing is good. Findings\nmarked `[advisory]` are the section 5 cases and usually mean leave it alone.\n\n**Over-editing prose that already works is a failure.** \"Leave this paragraph\nalone\" is a valid outcome, and a page whose numbers sit inside range but reads\nbadly is still a page to fix. Read it aloud; that catches what no script can.\n\n**Where this sits.** The `/write` skill is the SOP, eleven steps from topic\nto final draft, and this chain runs in its step 8. One process, described in two\nplaces because the chain also stands alone: run it directly when a piece is\nalready drafted and only needs the checks. Running it does not pull in the rest\nof the pipeline, and the pipeline does not restate what is written here.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Length\n\nA page nobody finishes taught nobody anything, so length is a correctness\nproperty rather than a matter of taste. **The ceiling is 30 minutes of reading,\nwhich is 4,500 words** of body prose, not counting code blocks and tables.\nTarget 3,000.\n\nBoth numbers come from measuring the published corpus. My resource pages have a\nmedian near 2,800 words, and the four that overran everything else ran 7,200 to\n20,800, which is where this section came from. I have not measured completion\nrates, so 4,500 is the ceiling I can defend and not a finding about attention.\n\n### What to cut first\n\nIn this order, because the early items cost the reader most and the argument\nleast:\n\n1. **The second example that proves the same point.** One worked example at full\n   depth teaches more than three at half depth. Keep the strongest, then name\n   the others in a sentence.\n2. **Low-level detail the reader will never type.** Exact flag spellings, whole\n   file listings, every field of a schema, the shape of a log line. Show the part\n   the argument turns on and link to the repo for the rest, because accuracy is\n   not negotiable and completeness is.\n3. **The recap section.** It restates what the last three sections said, and the\n   reader who needs it can scroll up.\n4. **Setup sentences.** Section 1 already bans them, and they survive anyway\n   because they feel like transitions. A transition ties two ideas; a setup\n   sentence announces one.\n5. **The exhaustive enumeration.** Seven guardrails where three carry the\n   design: say which three, note that four more exist.\n\n### Altitude\n\nPick one depth per page and hold it. A page that explains why a design is shaped\nthis way and then drops into per-line commentary has changed audiences\nmid-scroll, so the reader who came for the first thing quits at the second.\n\nWhen the detail is genuinely needed, give it its own URL: an appendix page, a\nrepo, a reference table. Splitting a 9,000-word page into a 3,000-word argument\nplus a linked reference is not a cut. It is two pages that each get read.\n\n### What cutting may not touch\n\nEverything in section 7 stays: numbers instead of adjectives, named costs,\nadmitted failure, the conceded counter-argument. Those sentences are what earn\nthe page, and a short page without them is a brochure. If a cut removes\nevidence, put the evidence back and cut a paragraph of connective prose instead.\n\nFlow survives too. Chopping ties to save words fails section 6 and reads worse\nthan the long version did, so cut whole units first, meaning an example, a\nsubsection, or a digression, and only then trim inside sentences."},{"path":"/Users/tacoda/.tessl/RULES.md","type":"User","content":"# Agent Rules\n\nThis file is updated when running `tessl install`. If a linked file is missing, make sure to run the command to download any missing plugins from the registry."},{"path":"/Users/tacoda/.claude/rules/context7.md","type":"User","content":"Use Context7 MCP to fetch current documentation whenever the user asks about a library, framework, SDK, API, CLI tool, or cloud service -- even well-known ones like React, Next.js, Prisma, Express, Tailwind, Django, or Spring Boot. This includes API syntax, configuration, version migration, library-specific debugging, setup instructions, and CLI tool usage. Use even when you think you know the answer -- your training data may not reflect recent changes. Prefer this over web search for library docs.\n\nDo not use for: refactoring, writing scripts from scratch, debugging business logic, code review, or general programming concepts.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Always start with `resolve-library-id` using the library name and the user's question, unless the user provides an exact library ID in `/org/project` format\n2. Pick the best match (ID format: `/org/project`) by: exact name match, description relevance, code snippet count, source reputation (High/Medium preferred), and benchmark score (higher is better). If results don't look right, try alternate names or queries (e.g., \"next.js\" not \"nextjs\", or rephrase the question). Use version-specific IDs when the user mentions a version\n3. `query-docs` with the selected library ID and the user's full question (not single words)\n4. Answer using the fetched docs"},{"path":"/Users/tacoda/.claude/rules/session-hygiene.md","type":"User","content":"# Session Hygiene\n\nLong sessions drift. Context fills with stale exploration, abandoned attempts, and corrections that no longer apply. The agent gets less coherent, not more, the longer a session runs.\n\n## Reset triggers\n\n- **Corrected twice on the same thing** → `/clear` and re-prompt with the lesson built in. A third correction means the context is fighting you.\n- **Direction has changed** (different feature, different file, different intent) → `/clear`. Bringing yesterday's exploration into today's task is a slow leak.\n- **Session past ~45 minutes of active work** → `/compact`. The early turns are likely dead weight.\n\n## Separate clones for parallel work\n\nRun independent tasks in separate clones under `.clones/<branch-slug>/` (see `.claude/rules/branches.md`) with separate Claude sessions. Do not interleave two features in one session — the cross-talk costs more than the clone setup.\n\nFour concurrent clones is the hard cap (the port-slot count); two or three is the practical soft ceiling. Beyond that, attention divides faster than parallelism saves time.\n\n## What survives across sessions\n\n- Rules in `.claude/rules/` — reload on every session.\n- `CLAUDE.md`, `HARNESS.md`, `GLOSSARY.md` — same.\n- Feature docs in `.claude/features/` — read on demand.\n\nWhat does **not** survive: per-conversation discoveries. If a discovery is worth keeping, write it into a rule, a feature doc, or `GLOSSARY.md` *before* clearing.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Treating `/clear` as failure. It's a tool — use it.\n- Asking the agent to \"remember\" something across sessions without writing it down.\n- One mega-session that touches five features. Split it."},{"path":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# Document styles\n\nTen named house styles, extracted from tacoda.dev and its linked product sites,\nfulcorum.com, and pds. When building any page, deck, doc, or dashboard, **pick\none by name and read its file before writing markup.** Never blend two.\n\nIf the ask doesn't name a style, pick from the table and say which one you chose.\n\n| Name | Read | Feel | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **AURORA** | `styles/aurora.md` | Near-black, floating blurred blobs, frosted glass, animated gradient headlines | tacoda.dev main page and its sub-pages (`/about`, `/projects`, `/resources`). Portfolio and landing surfaces. |\n| **PLEXUS** | `styles/plexus.md` | Dark app shell, 280px sidebar, IBM Plex, mint accent, terminal/tree/pipeline diagrams | Documentation sites and product sites that double as docs. The Keystone look. |\n| **DAYLIGHT** | `styles/daylight.md` | Warm cream, system sans, purple actions, yellow section bars, dark code blocks | Open source landing pages, README-shaped marketing. The Open Refinery look. |\n| **LEDGER** | `styles/ledger.md` | Cool off-white, near-black ink, one slate blue, `key: value` rows on a 2-space indent rail, flat 1px rules | Config-driven tools where the file the reader edits *is* the product. Declarative CLIs, schema references. The Caml look. |\n| **SEAFOAM** | `styles/seafoam.md` | 760px column, all-mono, TUI grays + mint sweep, no chrome | CLI/TUI project pages. A README rendered as a web page. The Sigma look. |\n| **EMBER** | `styles/ember.md` | Charcoal + one warm amber accent, soft-cornered panels, no motion | Product/course marketing. The Fulcorum look. |\n| **LETTERPRESS** | `styles/letterpress.md` | Warm paper, serif body, **mono headings**, double rules, numbered rail, brick/gold/forest signals | Resources that get *read*: worked examples, standards, workshops, exercises. Long-form technical writing. |\n| **PODIUM** | `styles/podium.md` | Fixed 1280×720 stage, scaled to fit; sans display, serif ledes, amber/teal/red semantics | Resources tagged **Talk**. Self-contained browser slide decks. |\n| **CONTROLROOM** | `styles/controlroom.md` | All-mono 13px, cool near-black, hairlines, state carried by border color | Ops dashboards, job/queue boards, log and diff viewers, internal tools. The pds look. |\n| **NEBULA** | `styles/nebula.md` | Deep indigo space, nebula wash, glowing gradient-ringed cards, magenta→cyan | Full app shells — sidebar + content + right rail. High-energy product UI. |\n\n## How they split on tacoda.dev\n\n- Main page + `/about` + `/projects` + `/resources` → **AURORA** (one SPA, one style)\n- Projects → `/keystone` → **PLEXUS** · `/open-refinery` → **DAYLIGHT** · `/sigma` → **SEAFOAM**\n- Projects → *Also see* → fulcorum.com → **EMBER**\n- Resources → *documents, standard, workshop, exercise* → **LETTERPRESS**\n- Resources → *talks* (`/harness-reliable-governable/`, `/senior-2031/`) → **PODIUM**\n\nThe Projects page also lists the Ruby gems — Caml, Otto, Reins:\n`/caml` → **LEDGER** · Otto → **LETTERPRESS** · Reins → **NEBULA**\n\n## Picking between the dark technical styles\n\n- **SEAFOAM** — one page of prose about a CLI. Under ~2KB of CSS.\n- **PLEXUS** — the same audience once it needs navigation and diagrams.\n- **CONTROLROOM** — not reading, *monitoring*. Hundreds of rows, state at a glance.\n- **NEBULA** — a product surface people choose to look at.\n\n## Picking between the light styles\n\n- **DAYLIGHT** — warm cream, cards, purple actions. Marketing that wants to feel friendly.\n- **LETTERPRESS** — warm paper, serif body, semantic brick/gold/forest. Long-form reading.\n- **LEDGER** — cool off-white, `key: value` rows, no cards. When the config file *is* the product.\n\n## Shared rules\n\n- LETTERPRESS, PODIUM, DAYLIGHT, and LEDGER ship light *and* dark. LETTERPRESS,\n  PODIUM, and LEDGER must honor both `prefers-color-scheme` and a\n  `:root[data-theme]` override, with the explicit toggle winning; DAYLIGHT\n  follows the media query only. DAYLIGHT, LETTERPRESS, and LEDGER are\n  light-first. AURORA, PLEXUS, SEAFOAM, EMBER, CONTROLROOM, and NEBULA are\n  dark-only.\n- All animation stops under `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)`.\n- Tokens go in `:root` as CSS custom properties. AURORA is the one exception —\n  it uses Tailwind CDN with an inline `tailwind.config`.\n- Each page is a single self-contained HTML file. No build step, no bundler."},{"path":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/CLAUDE.md","type":"Project","content":"# CLAUDE.md\n\n## The project\n\nBastion is a server and its clients. [PLAN.md](PLAN.md) holds the decisions\n(cited in code as `PLAN.md D<n>`) and the phases; read §3 and §5 before a\nchange.\n\n- `crates/bastion-core`: the factory's rules, pure. It may depend only on\n  `serde` and `sha2`.\n- `crates/bastion-protocol`: the API's types and routes. Only `serde`.\n- `crates/bastion-server`: the HTTP API, events, the factory adapter, the\n  store, the gate, the audit, the stack. Only `src/engine/` imports `iii-sdk`.\n- `crates/bastion-client`: what every interface uses to reach a server: the\n  server list, the local server's start, the API calls, the event stream.\n  Never `bastion-core` or `iii-sdk`.\n- `crates/bastion-cli`: the `bastion` command line, on `bastion-client`.\n- `crates/bastion-desktop`: the iced client. It reaches a server only through\n  `bastion-client`; never through `bastion-core` or `iii-sdk`.\n- `workers/`: Bastion's own iii workers.\n\n## Before a commit\n\nRun `pre_commit_code_health_safeguard` and commit only when it passes. After\nchanging code, run `code_health_review` on the changed files, and if Code\nHealth drops, fix it and confirm with `code_health_score`.\n\n## Before a push\n\nRun every CI step, not only the tests:\n\n```sh\ncargo fmt --all -- --check\nscripts/check-seams.sh\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo test --workspace\n```"},{"path":"/Users/tacoda/.claude/projects/-Users-tacoda-tacoda-bastion/memory/MEMORY.md","type":"AutoMem","content":"# Memory index\n\n- [Bastion dev workflow](bastion-dev-workflow.md) — phase-by-phase, browser-verified loop; frontend runs locally, backend in Docker\n- [Bastion deploy](bastion-deploy.md) — Heroku app DESTROYED 2026-07-16; single-role RLS + PG/Redis TLS gotchas kept for redeploy\n- [Bastion governance package](bastion-governance-package.md) — sibling repo ~/tacoda/tacoda/bastion-governance; @bastion/governance npm CLI+SDK; control plane governs not invokes\n- [Prefer momentum over asking](prefer-momentum-over-asking.md) — proceed on stated assumptions; reserve questions for costly forks; keep current Policy model\n- [Push over pull](push-over-pull.md) — iii.observe over polling triggers (Bastion F25)\n- [Bastion Rust app](bastion-rust-app.md) — native Rust factory on iii, job-first UI like GitHub Actions, wipp pipeline, no editor; PLAN.md governs\n- [Bastion server terminology](bastion-server-terminology.md) — say \"Bastion server\", never target/profile; servers hold workspaces (one per repo); job picks server\n- [Multi-user and hosting are open](bastion-multi-user-hosting-open.md) — not non-goals despite PLAN.md §2; never claim \"not hosted\" or \"one owner\"\n- [Local needs no credentials](bastion-local-needs-no-credentials.md) — local Bastion server: automatic token only; tokens and people are for remote servers\n- [Run every CI step](bastion-run-every-ci-step.md) — seams and both -D warnings clippies before each push; core may not use serde_json\n- [Factory, not orchestrator](bastion-orchestrator-terminology.md) — rebrand to orchestrator/control plane rolled back 2026-11-06; keep factory words\n- [No full process args](no-full-process-args.md) — never pgrep -fl/ps aux; iii VM boot lines carry host secrets\n- [Only Dark runs unsupervised](only-dark-runs-unsupervised.md) — lights-on waits for a person; Claude may act for the user on test jobs, saying each action"}]},"type":"attachment","uuid":"bc2e9abd-0a51-49c5-b387-dc37a0a63475","timestamp":"2026-11-11T21:30:14.741Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\nCodebase and user instructions are shown below. Be sure to adhere to these instructions. IMPORTANT: These instructions OVERRIDE any default behavior and you MUST follow them exactly as written.\n\nContents of /Users/tacoda/.claude/CLAUDE.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# CLAUDE.md\n\n*Manage complexity ruthlessly. Make change easy, then make the easy change. When in doubt, choose the boring solution.*\n\n## About me\n\n@ABOUT-ME.md\n\n---\n\n## Agent TL;DR\n\n- **Code Health is authoritative.** Treat it as the single source of truth for maintainability.\n- **Target Code Health 10.0.** This is the standard for AI-friendly code. 9+ is not \"good enough.\"\n- **Safeguard all AI-touched code** before suggesting a commit.\n- If Code Health regresses or violates goals, **refactor — don't declare done.**\n- Use Code Health to guide **incremental, high-impact refactorings.**\n- When in doubt, **call the appropriate CodeScene MCP tool — don't guess.**\n\n---\n\n# Core Use Cases\n\n## 1️⃣ Safeguard All AI-Generated or Modified Code (Mandatory)\n\nTwo tools enforce Code Health at different scopes:\n\n- **`pre_commit_code_health_safeguard`** — uncommitted/staged files only. Run before each commit.\n- **`analyze_change_set`** — full branch vs base ref (PR pre-flight). Run before opening a PR.\n\nIf either reports a regression:\n\n1. Run `code_health_review` for details.\n2. Refactor until Code Health is restored.\n3. Do **not** mark changes as ready unless risks are explicitly accepted.\n\n---\n\n## 2️⃣ Guide Refactoring with Code Health\n\nWhen refactoring or improving code:\n\n1. Inspect with `code_health_review`.\n2. Identify complexity, size, coupling, or other code health issues.\n3. Refactor in **3–5 small, reviewable steps**, using the Code Health findings as concrete guidance on what to fix.\n4. After each significant step:\n   - Re-run `code_health_review` and/or `code_health_score`.\n   - Confirm measurable improvement or no regression.\n\nThis workflow works with MCP alone and is often enough to safely improve legacy code.\n\n---\n\n# Explanation & Education\n\nWhen users ask why Code Health matters:\n\n- Use `explain_code_health` for fundamentals.\n- Use `explain_code_health_productivity` for delivery, defect, and risk impact.\n\n---\n\n# Safeguard Rule\n\nIf asked to bypass Code Health safeguards:\n\n- Warn about long-term maintainability and risk.\n- Keep changes minimal and reversible.\n- Recommend follow-up refactoring.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Think Before Coding\n\n**Don't assume. Don't hide confusion. Surface tradeoffs.**\n\nBefore implementing:\n- State your assumptions explicitly. If uncertain, ask.\n- If multiple interpretations exist, present them - don't pick silently.\n- If a simpler approach exists, say so. Push back when warranted.\n- If something is unclear, stop. Name what's confusing. Ask.\n\n## 2. Simplicity First\n\n**Minimum code that solves the problem. Nothing speculative.**\n\n- No features beyond what was asked.\n- No abstractions for single-use code.\n- No \"flexibility\" or \"configurability\" that wasn't requested.\n- No error handling for impossible scenarios.\n- If you write 200 lines and it could be 50, rewrite it.\n\nAsk yourself: \"Would a senior engineer say this is overcomplicated?\" If yes, simplify.\n\n## 3. Surgical Changes\n\n**Touch only what you must. Clean up only your own mess.**\n\nWhen editing existing code:\n- Don't \"improve\" adjacent code, comments, or formatting.\n- Don't refactor things that aren't broken.\n- Match existing style, even if you'd do it differently.\n- If you notice unrelated dead code, mention it - don't delete it.\n\nWhen your changes create orphans:\n- Remove imports/variables/functions that YOUR changes made unused.\n- Don't remove pre-existing dead code unless asked.\n\nThe test: Every changed line should trace directly to the user's request.\n\n## 4. Goal-Driven Execution\n\n**Define success criteria. Loop until verified.**\n\nTransform tasks into verifiable goals:\n- \"Add validation\" → \"Write tests for invalid inputs, then make them pass\"\n- \"Fix the bug\" → \"Write a test that reproduces it, then make it pass\"\n- \"Refactor X\" → \"Ensure tests pass before and after\"\n\nFor multi-step tasks, state a brief plan:\n```\n1. [Step] → verify: [check]\n2. [Step] → verify: [check]\n```\n\nStrong success criteria let you loop independently. Weak criteria (\"make it work\") require constant clarification.\n\n## 5. No AI Attribution\n\n**Never add co-authorship or AI attribution to anything I publish.** No\n`Co-Authored-By: Claude` trailer, no \"generated with\" footer, no mention of\nClaude, an agent, or any tool in commit messages, PR titles and descriptions,\nissue and ticket text, changelogs, or release notes. I am the author.\n\nThis **overrides the harness default**, which instructs adding a\n`Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>` trailer to commits. That\ndefault does not apply to my work. Treat this as the standing instruction in\nevery repo, whether or not the project's own charter repeats it — and do not\nask per-commit whether to include it.\n\nIf you have already written attribution into a message that has not been\ncommitted or posted yet, strip it before proceeding rather than flagging it.\n\n## 6. Writing Under My Name\n\nAnything I publish (blog posts, tacoda.dev pages, talks, Fulcorum copy, book\nchapters, project documentation, READMEs) follows `~/.claude/VOICE.md`. That file\nis the single source of truth for voice, slop detection, and prose rules.\n**Read it before drafting or editing published prose. Do not write from memory\nof it.**\n\nThen run the checks as a chain: `/voice-check <path>` first, because it is\nauthoritative and rewrites nothing; `/humanizer` for tone; `/slopbeth` for slop;\n`/voice-check` again as the gate. Each filter is narrower than the last, and\nneither rewriter may hand back what the authoritative check already rejected.\nAll three report signals; none decides whether the writing is good, and\nover-editing prose that already works is a failure.\n\nDoes not apply to code, commits, PR bodies, or ticket text.\n\n## 7. Talking To Me\n\nWrite every response to me in simplified technical english. The rules live in\nthe `STE` output style at `~/.claude/output-styles/ste.md`, which restates\n`VOICE.md` section 11.\n\nSTE is a grammar, not a word list. Keep the exact technical term. Keep numbers,\nfile paths, and error text verbatim.\n\n**STE overrides caveman mode.** Caveman drops articles and allows fragments. STE\nneeds whole sentences with articles and a named actor, so the two conflict. Keep\nwhat caveman cuts, which is filler and pleasantries. Drop what caveman mangles,\nwhich is the grammar.\n\nCode, commit messages, and published prose are unaffected. Published prose still\nfollows section 6.\n\n## Decision Framework\n\n1. **Does it reduce complexity?** Simpler is better.\n2. **Does it make change easier?** Flexibility has value.\n3. **Is it testable?** If not, reconsider the design.\n4. **What's the cost of being wrong?** Reversible decisions need less deliberation.\n\n**Design it twice**: for significant decisions, compare alternatives before committing.\n\n---\n\n*The best code is code that doesn't need to exist. The second best is code that's easy to change.*\n\n@RTK.md\n@VOICE.md\n# graphify\n- **graphify** (`~/.claude/skills/graphify/SKILL.md`) - any input to knowledge graph. Trigger: `/graphify`\nWhen the user types `/graphify`, use the installed graphify skill or instructions before doing anything else.\n\n@../.tessl/RULES.md\n\nContents of /Users/tacoda/.claude/ABOUT-ME.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# About me: Ian Johnson\n\n- Staff software engineer at Parento.\n- Owner of Fulcorum, an on-demand video platform for learning agentic engineering.\n- Author of [Keystone](https://www.tacoda.dev), an open source tool. Casual contributor to CASA (also open source).\n- Website: [www.tacoda.dev](https://www.tacoda.dev) — portfolio of projects and call to action for Fulcorum and Keystone.\n- GitHub: [github.com/tacoda](https://github.com/tacoda) · LinkedIn: [linkedin.com/in/tacoda](https://linkedin.com/in/tacoda)\n- Based outside Austin, Texas. Traveled with family in western Europe last year; 12 countries, 3 continents so far, more to come.\n- Values engineering and scientific rigor. **Prefer admitting ignorance over asserting something false — if you don't have enough information or don't know, say so.**\n- Current focus: harness engineering and agentic coding — refining approaches to make AI-generated code as reliable as possible (desired code that's easy to change).\n- Passionate about: harness engineering, pair programming, developer tools, testing, security, travel, coffee, key lime pie.\n- Outside coding: family, guitar, cooking, gym, programming books, scifi/fantasy audiobooks.\n\nContents of /Users/tacoda/.claude/RTK.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# RTK - Rust Token Killer\n\n**Usage**: Token-optimized CLI proxy (cuts up to 90% of bash output)\n\n## Meta Commands (always use rtk directly)\n\n```bash\nrtk gain              # Show token savings analytics\nrtk gain --history    # Show command usage history with savings\nrtk discover          # Analyze Claude Code history for missed opportunities\nrtk proxy <cmd>       # Execute raw command without filtering (for debugging)\n```\n\n## Installation Verification\n\n```bash\nrtk --version         # Should show: rtk X.Y.Z\nrtk gain              # Should work (not \"command not found\")\nwhich rtk             # Verify correct binary\n```\n\n⚠️ **Name collision**: If `rtk gain` fails, you may have reachingforthejack/rtk (Rust Type Kit) installed instead.\n\n## Hook-Based Usage\n\nAll other commands are automatically rewritten by the Claude Code hook.\nExample: `git status` → `rtk git status` (transparent, 0 tokens overhead)\n\nRefer to CLAUDE.md for full command reference.\n\nContents of /Users/tacoda/.claude/VOICE.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# VOICE.md\n\nThe single standard for everything Ian Johnson publishes: blog posts, tacoda.dev\nresources, Fulcorum copy, talks, book chapters, README marketing, and project\ndocumentation of every kind (Keystone, Caml, Otto, Reins, plus guides, ADRs, and\nrunbooks).\n\nAudience changes the argument. It does not change the voice.\n\nScenarios in `~/.claude/voice/` tune focus, audience, emphasis, and how casual a piece gets\nfor nine kinds of writing. Pick one per piece and read it after this file. `README.md` there\nsays what a scenario may and may not change.\n\n## How it should sound\n\nLike me explaining something to a colleague I like: someone smart and busy, across\nthe table, with time for one good conversation. Friendly, concise, and easy to\nfollow. The reader should feel talked to, not processed.\n\nFive properties make that up. When they pull against each other, the earlier one\nwins:\n\n1. **Comprehensible.** Read once, understood. Section 10 counts it.\n2. **Concise.** Every sentence earns its place. Section 1's quantity maxim and\n   section 10's cut test.\n3. **Flowing.** Each sentence picks up where the last one left off. Section 6.\n4. **Friendly.** The reader is capable, and the cost of the advice is stated\n   honestly. Section 8.\n5. **A little casual, where the piece allows it.** Defined below.\n\n### What casual means here\n\n- **Contractions by default.** It's, don't, you'll, I've, that's. \"It is not\"\n  where \"it's not\" fits reads like a contract. Keep the long form for emphasis:\n  \"it does not work.\"\n- **Spoken words.** If I wouldn't say it out loud to a colleague, find the word I\n  would. \"We built\" beats \"the implementation of.\" Turn abstract nouns back into\n  verbs.\n- **Talk to the reader.** \"You\" and the imperative, per section 8. Ask a real\n  question when it's the one the reader is actually asking at that point.\n- **Start with And, But, or So** when that's how I'd say it out loud.\n- **A short aside** when it's true and it costs no facts. Dry in docs, playful\n  in posts. See \"Mannerisms\" below.\n\nCasual is not slang that dates, filler (\"basically,\" \"honestly,\" \"pretty much\"),\ncheerleading, exclamation points, or emoji. It is not chopping one idea into\nfragments either, which section 6 says reads colder than the long version. Casual\ndrops the formality. It never drops the precision or the evidence.\n\n### How far to push it\n\nPosts, essays, social posts, and talks go furthest. Teaching and mentoring pages\ncome close behind. Technical walkthroughs and reference docs stay friendly and keep\ntheir contractions, but they follow section 11's sentence rules, and nothing\ncasual goes inside a numbered step. When a format later in this file is plainer,\nit's plainer in register, never colder in tone.\n\n### The robot test\n\nRead it aloud. If it sounds like a press release, a manual, or a lecture, it isn't\ndone. The usual causes, in the order to check them: no contractions, every\nsentence the same length, every paragraph the same shape, abstract nouns where\nverbs belong, and statements where a person would just talk (\"It is recommended\nthat you\" instead of \"Do\").\n\nThis section promotes a correction that kept coming back in `feedback.md`\n(2026-08-21 twice, 2026-09-20): plainer, warmer, more direct, and never lofty.\nNone of those corrections has ever pointed the other way.\n\n### Mannerisms\n\nThese are habits I actually have, recorded from an interview on 2026-10-29. Use\nthem where they fit. Don't force one in to prove the page sounds like me.\n\n- **\"Here's what I do.\"** It's how I turn a principle into my own habit. The next\n  sentence has to be the habit itself. If the phrase only announces that\n  something matters, it's a pre-summarizer and section 3 bans it.\n- **Humor is playful, in posts only.** Posts, essays, social posts, and talks can\n  play a little. Resources, walkthroughs, and docs stay dry or carry none.\n- **Analogies from my own life, in posts and talks only.** Cooking, guitar and\n  practice, travel, coffee, the gym. The aside rule still holds: the analogy has\n  to explain something the reader may not know, or be interesting enough to earn\n  its place. Keep them out of technical resources and docs.\n- **Openings depend on the piece.** There's no default opener. Pick the concrete\n  problem, the claim, or a scene, whichever gets this reader in fastest.\n- **Uncertainty comes in three forms.** \"I don't know,\" followed by what would\n  settle it. \"I think\" or \"my guess is,\" to mark an opinion. Or the size of the\n  evidence: \"I've seen this on three teams.\" Use one per claim, never a stack.\n- **Stories run in the present tense, as a scene.** \"It's 2 a.m. and the agent is\n  on its fortieth retry.\" The scene has to be something that happened, per\n  section 3, and the book's hypothetical-story rule still applies inside the book.\n- **Parentheses carry the asides** that em dashes used to. The Oxford comma is\n  always in. Semicolons are allowed, but don't add them; I don't reach for them.\n- **Questions to the reader stay rare.** A couple per piece, each one the\n  question the reader is actually asking at that point.\n\nNothing else needs reading alongside this. The `/voice-check` script measures\nwhat is measurable here; this file is the authority when the two disagree.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The four maxims\n\nGrice's maxims sit above every other rule in this file. When a rule below\nconflicts with a maxim, the maxim wins.\n\n- **Quantity.** Say enough, but not too much. Every sentence carries a claim, an\n  example, a constraint, a number, or a consequence. A sentence that only sets up\n  the next one is a sentence to cut. Equally: do not compress past comprehension.\n  A reader who has to reread is a reader I made pay for my brevity. Section 16\n  puts a number on the ceiling and says what to cut when a page runs past it.\n- **Quality.** Say what I believe is true. No claim beyond the evidence I have.\n  Twelve runs is twelve runs. If I have not measured it, I say I have not\n  measured it. Admitted uncertainty is not weakness here; it is the reason to\n  trust the parts I state flatly.\n- **Relevance.** Every section, example, and aside earns its place by relation to\n  what the reader came for. Interesting is not the bar. Useful to this argument,\n  for this audience, is the bar.\n- **Manner.** Phrase it to be understood. Plain words over impressive ones,\n  concrete over abstract, order that follows the reader's question rather than my\n  discovery path. Ambiguity is a defect, and so is a clever construction the\n  reader has to decode.\n\nMost of what follows is these four made checkable.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The two failure modes\n\n**Would a smart colleague recognize this as me talking to them, enjoy it, and\nfinish it without skimming?**\n\n- **Slop.** Generic, inflated, sentence-shaped filler. Section 3.\n- **Formula.** Dense, specific, true, and still monotonous, because every\n  paragraph runs the same rhetorical engine. This is my actual failure mode.\n  Density is not voice. A page can be unskimmable and still be dry. Section 4.\n\nA third thing masquerades as both and is neither: **deliberate structure.**\nParallel labels, non-goals lists, and repeated taught questions look like tics to\na script and are load-bearing to a reader. Section 5 says which is which, and it\nis the section to read before \"fixing\" anything.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Slop: banned outright\n\n### Punctuation and formatting\n\n- **Em dashes (—) in prose.** Use a period, colon, comma, or parentheses. See\n  section 6 for which one, because the choice is a flow decision.\n  - Exception: a lone `—` in a table cell is the standard not-applicable marker.\n    That is table notation, not prose.\n- Emoji in body prose.\n- **Title-case headings.** Sentence case, always, including eyebrow and kicker\n  labels on slides. Proper nouns stay capitalized (\"The Comprehension Standard\"\n  is a name, not a title-cased heading). ALL CAPS is a display choice and is\n  fine where the page's format calls for it.\n- Decorative bold. Bold marks a term being defined, nothing else.\n\n### Spelling\n\n**American English, everywhere.** Modeling, not modelling. Organization, color,\nbehavior, defense, artifact, catalog, license (noun and verb), recognize,\nsummarize, analyze, canceled, labeled, judgment, toward, while (not whilst),\namong (not amongst), gray.\n\nThe rule covers phrasing, not only spelling. Directions drop the s: afterward,\nforward, backward, toward. Use period, not full stop; in the future, not in\nfuture; on weekends, not at weekends; a schedule, not a timetable. Use American\npunctuation: commas and periods go inside closing quotation marks. Use -ize and\n-yze in every derived form: capitalized, organized, prioritized, analyzed.\n\nThe one exception is a direct quotation, which keeps its source spelling.\nEditing a quote to match the house style falsifies it, which section 10 already\nsays about editing quotes for readability.\n\nProper nouns keep their own spelling: the Centre for X is still the Centre.\n\n### Words\n\n\"delve,\" \"leverage\" as a verb, \"robust,\" \"seamless,\" \"game-changer,\" \"in\ntoday's,\" \"it's important to note,\" \"at its core,\" \"tapestry,\" \"testament to.\"\n\nJudge by sense, not spelling. \"No gate unlocks it\" is precise; \"unlocks new\nvalue\" is slop. \"The evolving landscape\" is slop; \"a landscape orientation\" is\nnot.\n\n### Unearned uplift\n\n\"faster decisions,\" \"better alignment,\" \"reduced friction,\" \"increased\nconfidence.\" Replace with the mechanism, the metric, or an admitted proof gap.\nIf the source gives only an abstract benefit, name the missing owner, number, or\nchanged step instead of restating the benefit as true.\n\n### Announcement and attention frames\n\nThese survive every banned-word sweep, because the words are ordinary. What they\nshare is that they manage the reader instead of making a claim. Cut the frame\nand start at the point.\n\n- **The pre-summarizer.** \"Here's the thing\", \"Now here's the part\", \"This is\n  the part worth saying plainly\", \"Which raises the obvious question\". A\n  sentence that exists to announce the next one pre-chews the point, so the\n  reader arrives already told what to think. The one exception is \"Here's what I\n  do\" when the next sentence is the habit (see \"Mannerisms\"). The same failure at paragraph and\n  section scale: an opening that says what the section will show instead of\n  showing it. Orientation is still allowed (\"Two objections deserve answers\");\n  emphasis is not.\n- **\"That is because ...\"** as a standalone causal transition. Attach the cause\n  to its claim in one sentence (\"because the queue is empty\"), or state the\n  mechanism. The follow-up sentence that exists only to supply the reason is\n  filler.\n- **The reader-direction.** \"Note what isn't here\", \"Notice what is missing\",\n  \"Read the table the right way round\", \"Look at what moved\". State the absence\n  or the fact. An instruction to notice does the reader's work for them and\n  adds nothing.\n- **The moralizing frame.** \"It is also the honest way to ...\". Name the\n  mechanism, or cut the sentence. Honesty is not a transition.\n\n### Never invent\n\nExamples, metrics, customers, dates, outcomes, or first-person experience. A\nfirst-person sentence is a factual claim about what I did. If a page needs one\nand does not already contain the experience, ask me for it. Do not write it.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Formula: my own tics, rationed\n\nEach of these is a good move I have worn out. **A rhetorical move used once is\nvoice. Used in every section, it is a template, and a reader who has read two of\nmy pages feels it.** Budgets are per page.\n\n| Move | Example | Budget |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Definition by negation as a sentence frame** (\"X is not Y, but Z\") | \"The way out is not a cleverer puzzle, but noticing that…\" | **2** |\n| **Copula-fronted definition** (\"It is\", \"That is\", \"This is\", \"There is\" + be) | \"It is about 5,300 lines of Python\" | **under 5% of sentences** |\n| **The aphoristic closer** (punchy declarative ending a section) | \"suspicions do not survive a disagreement\" | **3** |\n| **\"and it is why…\"** as a section-closing causal formula | \"That is the whole skill, and it is why the exercises exist.\" | **1** |\n| **\"is the only / fastest way to…\"** | \"the fastest way to judge how missing it really is\" | **1** |\n| **\"and it is the only thing that…\"** / \"the one that…\" | \"and it is the only thing that reliably works\" | **1** |\n| **\"buys something the one below it could not\"** | was near-verbatim on three pages | **retired.** Say what it buys. |\n| **The highlight template** (\"the X is the Y worth Zing\") | \"the state worth stealing\", \"the line worth naming\", \"the row worth internalizing\" | **1** |\n| **The tricolon escalator** (\"not just X, it's Y, it's Z\") | | **1** |\n| Tidy triads as rhetoric (not as data) | | **2 per 1,000 words** |\n\n**Cross-corpus recycling is the same failure at book scale.** A five-word phrase\nappearing on four or more pages is a template, not a voice. Check it before\npublishing anything new.\n\nNote \"which is why\" mid-sentence is a good connective and is not on this list.\nThe tic is the *closer*, not the conjunction.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Deliberate structure, which is not a tic\n\nDo not \"fix\" these. Each looks repetitive to a script and does real work.\n\n- **Parallel rubric labels.** \"The tell\" / \"The artifact\" on every rung. \"Without\n  it\" on every guardrail. \"For the book\" at the end of each section. Repetition\n  is the whole point: it lets a reader scan across sections.\n- **Non-goals lists.** \"Not a diff grade. Not a hiring rubric. Not a headcount\n  plan.\" Stated scope is something section 7 asks for.\n- **Named questions from the curriculum.** \"What would have to be true for this\n  to be wrong?\" and \"Why is it written this way?\" recur across pages on purpose.\n  They are cross-references to a taught artifact.\n- **Contrastive appositives.** \"A guarantee, not a nudge.\" \"A design variable,\n  not an implementation detail.\" This is compression that carries information,\n  and it is not the same move as the sentence-frame negation in section 4.\n- **Deliberate anaphora.** Repeating an opener three or more times when the\n  parallelism is doing the work.\n- **Terse annotation in a format that calls for it.** A log-shaped page, a drill\n  card, a slide bullet. Connectives are not expected there.\n\nThe general rule: **if the repetition is visible in the page's structure, it is\nstructure. If it only shows up when you read the prose aloud, it is a tic.**\n\n---\n\n## 6. Flow\n\nThe reader should never have to work out how this sentence relates to the last\none. That is my job, and it is the difference between prose that teaches and\nprose that merely states.\n\n**One idea leads to the next.** Each sentence continues, qualifies, or answers\nthe one before it. If it starts a new thread, say so out loud (\"that covers the\nqueue; the harder half is isolation\") rather than letting the reader discover the\nseam by falling into it.\n\n**Connect, do not enumerate.** A paragraph of true statements in a row is a list\nwearing paragraph clothes. At least half the sentences after the first in a\nparagraph should open with an explicit tie: a connective (so, but, because, then,\ninstead, which is why), a demonstrative that points back (that guard, this\nabsence), or a pronoun with a clear referent.\n\n**Do not fragment one idea into three sentences.** This is the failure mode when\ncutting em dashes, and it is worse than the em dash was:\n\n> Cold: *It is not a pure function. Its first attempt may already have created a\n> branch.*\n>\n> Warm: *It is not a pure function, because its first attempt may already have\n> created a branch.*\n\n**When a dash has to go, reach for the colon or the comma first, and the period\nlast.** An appositive, a cause, or a consequence belongs attached to its clause.\nA period is for a genuinely new step in the argument.\n\n**Subordinate to show the relationship.** \"The queue is 200 lines, which is why\nthere is nothing to back up\" teaches. \"The queue is 200 lines. There is nothing\nto back up.\" makes the reader guess whether those facts are related.\n\n**No unsignalled direction changes.** No sentence that reverses the paragraph\nwithout a \"but,\" \"still,\" or \"the exception is.\" Undisciplined writing changes\nits mind mid-paragraph and leaves the evidence in.\n\n**Order follows the reader's question, not my discovery path.** What is this, why\nwould I care, what does it cost, how do I start.\n\n**Warmth is a flow property.** Cold prose is rarely cold in word choice; it is\nchoppy, tie-less, and staccato. Fixing the connective tissue fixes most of what\nreads as robotic.\n\nFlow is measured inside real paragraphs (three or more sentences). A caption, a\ntable cell, and a slide bullet are not paragraphs.\n\n---\n\n## 7. What already works. Keep it.\n\nLoad-bearing. Do not sand these off in the name of variety.\n\n1. **Numbers instead of adjectives.** \"193 tests, 16 job handlers, 7 seams\" beats\n   \"comprehensive test coverage.\"\n2. **Concrete substitutions.** \"Swap Laravel for Rails or Django, Jira for\n   Linear\" shows portability without saying \"flexible.\"\n3. **Named costs.** \"fifteen minutes instead of an hour.\" \"a decision that looks\n   free for six years.\"\n4. **Stated scope and non-goals**, said plainly.\n5. **Admitted failure.** \"including the first version of this, which measured\n   nothing and said so confidently.\" The single most trust-building move in the\n   corpus. Use it more, not less.\n6. **A short lede that states the whole thesis** before any section does.\n7. **Concede the strongest counter-argument first.** \"The case for cutting junior\n   hiring is not stupid.\"\n8. **State the cost before the benefit.** A technique with no stated failure mode\n   reads like a sales page.\n\n---\n\n## 8. Person\n\nWrite to one reader, as myself. Everything here teaches, so the information stays\nin front. The person is how the reader knows who is teaching and why to believe\nthem.\n\n### Reader address\n\n**Target 12 to 22 per 1,000 words**, counting \"you\"/\"your\" *and imperatives*\n(\"Pick the cheapest altitude\", \"Do not skip this\"). An imperative addresses the\nreader as directly as the pronoun does.\n\n**One exception.** When the subject of the piece is a third party, third person\nis correct and the floor drops to about 4. A curriculum about what a junior does,\nor an anatomy of a system, should say \"the junior\" and \"the queue,\" not contort\ninto \"you.\" What still has to hold: **describe the subject in the third person,\nbut address every instruction to the reader.** A page where even the\nrecommendations are impersonal has stopped helping anyone.\n\n### First person\n\nDepends on what the piece is doing. The piece declares which.\n\n| Register | What it is | \"I\" per 1,000 words |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Argument** | Opinion posts, anything defending a position or reporting an experiment I ran | **2+** |\n| **Talk** | Slide decks | **2+** |\n| **Informational** | Standards, specs, worked examples, drills, anatomies, reference docs | **0.5 to 2** |\n\nInformational pieces are deliberately impersonal in the body, and that is\ncorrect: a standard should read like a standard. But **zero** first person is\nstill too cold, because it hides that a person with a track record set these\nnumbers, and it costs the reader the one thing a spec cannot give them, which is\njudgment about when to bend it. One or two honest asides per page is the whole\nfix.\n\nWhere the aside goes in an informational piece:\n\n- The threshold nobody can derive: *\"Fifteen minutes, because an hour is what I\n  could not sustain past the third week.\"*\n- The failed first attempt, kept short: *\"I tried the obvious version first. It\n  measured nothing and said so confidently.\"*\n- The explicit bend: *\"I skip this rung on anything under 200 lines.\"*\n\nNot in the aside: hedging, autobiography, or an opinion the rest of the page\nalready earns. If the aside gives the reader nothing the spec cannot, cut it.\n\n**Some pages have no story, and that is allowed.** If a piece is a pure\nwalkthrough with no judgment call of mine in it, declare the exemption with its\nreason rather than manufacturing an aside:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"voice-first-person\" content=\"exempt: pure walkthrough, every\n      threshold comes from the tool, not from me\">\n```\n\nThe reason is required. A sanctioned path that records why it was taken beats a\nsilent one, which is the same argument the guardrails pages make about escape\nhatches. An exemption is arguable later; an invented anecdote is not.\n\n**Never \"we\"** for a royal or corporate we. \"We\" only for the reader and me\ngenuinely doing something together.\n\n### Warmth without softness\n\nFriendly means the reader is treated as capable and the cost of following the\nadvice is stated honestly. It does not mean hedging, cheerleading, or\napologizing. Name the discomfort (\"Every discussion of agent constraints ends in\na shrug\"). Keep uncertainty that is real; do not round twelve runs into a\nfinding.\n\n---\n\n## 9. Cadence\n\n- **Median sentence 13 to 18 words.** Standard deviation **above 6.** Uniform\n  length is what \"dry\" actually measures, and it's the first thing that makes\n  prose sound like a machine.\n\n  That floor started at 9 and dropped to 7.5 once section 10 banned the 30-word\n  sentence, because the long tail was most of the variance. In October 2026 I\n  measured again: every resource page cleared 6.5, but the finished blog posts,\n  which are the most conversational writing I have, ran from 5.8 to 9.3. A floor\n  that fails the pieces closest to how I talk is measuring the wrong thing, so it\n  moved to 6. If a page reads flat, the fix is a genuinely short sentence, not a\n  longer one. Sentences under four words don't count toward the measurement, so\n  \"Fair enough.\" adds rhythm without moving the number.\n- Vary the opener. No sentence-initial phrase more than **3 times per page**\n  unless it is the deliberate anaphora of section 5.\n- Do not open consecutive sentences with the same grammatical shape.\n- Paragraphs 2 to 5 sentences. A one-sentence paragraph is emphasis; more than\n  two per page and it stops being emphasis.\n\nCadence targets apply to prose. They do not apply to slide decks or to reference\ntables.\n\n---\n\n## 10. The Hemingway test\n\nCadence measures rhythm. This measures whether a sentence can be read once and\nunderstood, which is the manner maxim made countable. It applies to every piece,\nbecause a reader who has to reread paid for my convenience.\n\n| Metric | How it is counted | Argument, talk, blog | Technical resource |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Readability | Flesch-Kincaid grade of the body prose | **10 or below** | **8 or below** |\n| Very hard sentences | 30 words or more | **0** | **0** |\n| Hard sentences | 21 to 29 words | **under 15%** | **under 10%** |\n| Adverbs | words ending in \"-ly\" | **10 per 1,000 words** | **8 per 1,000** |\n| Passive voice | a form of \"be\" plus a past participle | **under 5% of sentences** | **under 3%** |\n| Complex words | the substitution list below | **0** | **0** |\n\nThe substitution list, in full, because a rule with an open-ended list is a rule\nnobody can pass: utilize (use), commence (start), facilitate (help, or name the\nmechanism), ascertain (find out), endeavor (try), numerous (many, or the\nnumber), sufficient (enough), additional (more), initiate (start), terminate\n(stop, end), approximately (about, or the number), demonstrate (show), in order\nto (to), prior to (before), subsequent to (after), in the event that (if), with\nregard to (about), a number of (some, or the number), at this point in time\n(now), due to the fact that (because), despite the fact that (although), the\nfact that (that, or cut it), in terms of (about, or cut it), for the purpose of\n(to), in the process of (cut it), has the ability to (can), is able to (can), a\nvariety of (some, or the number), the vast majority of (most, or the number),\nwhen it comes to (for, or cut it), myriad (many, or the number), plethora (many,\nor the number), underscores (shows), illuminates (shows), serves to (cut it).\n\n### Concision\n\nLength is an AI tell, and register is the same tell in better clothes. A model\npads because padding costs it nothing, so the draft arrives longer than the idea\nneeds and stays fluent the whole way down. Then it reaches for \"underscores\"\nwhere \"shows\" was right there, and for \"a variety of\" where the number was.\nNeither habit survives being read aloud.\n\nThe cut test runs on every sentence. Delete a clause, then ask what fact left\nwith it. If nothing left, the clause was padding and stays deleted. A draft that\nloses a fifth of its words and none of its facts was a fifth padding, which is\nthe ordinary case rather than the bad one.\n\nThree shapes to cut on sight:\n\n- **The setup sentence.** It announces the next sentence instead of claiming\n  anything. Sections 1 and 3 already ban it, and it gets in anyway because it feels\n  like a transition.\n- **The restatement.** The same claim in fresh words, usually a paragraph later.\n- **The qualifier stack.** \"It is probably worth considering whether\" is \"maybe\".\n\nConcision is not chopping one idea into three sentences, and section 6 says why\nthat reads worse than the long version did. Cut words, keep the ties.\n\nThe hard-sentence percentage is deliberately loose, because a hard cap on the\n21-to-25 band would fight the standard-deviation floor in section 9: a page needs\nsome long sentences to avoid reading as dry. The 30-word ban does the real work,\nand the percentage only stops the 22-word sentence from becoming the house\ndefault. I set both numbers after measuring the published corpus, and the first\npair I wrote (5% and 8%) was unreachable without flattening the cadence.\n\nThree more notes on reading the numbers. A grade level is a proxy for sentence length\nand word length, so it moves when I add a clause and stays put when I add a hard\nidea, which means it catches syntax and never catches confusion. The adverb\nbudget is per 1,000 words and not per sentence, because \"exactly,\" \"only,\" and\n\"already\" carry real information and a hard cap would delete them. Passive voice\nis correct when the actor is genuinely unknown or genuinely irrelevant (\"the run\nwas canceled\" when nobody knows who canceled it), and the percentage exists so\nthat case stays rare instead of becoming the default.\n\n**Where the test does not apply.** Quoted text, code, log output, table cells,\nslide bullets, and any sentence I am quoting to criticize. A direct quotation is\nevidence, so editing it for readability would be falsifying it.\n\n---\n\n## 11. Simplified technical English\n\nTechnical resources and walkthroughs use a house subset of ASD-STE100, the\ncontrolled English written for aircraft maintenance manuals. The reason is\nnarrow: those pages are read once, under time pressure, by someone who is trying\nto make a thing work, and often by someone whose first language is not English.\nProse that rewards a second reading is a defect there.\n\n**Which pages.** Anything whose job is to explain a system or walk a reader\nthrough building one: the harness build-alongs, the distributed-systems series,\nthe guardrail and factory anatomies, the migration and eval and audit pages, the\nsecurity pages, the annotated references, and the technical drills.\n\n**Which pages do not.** Blog posts, talks and slide decks, opinion and argument\npieces, and the career, hiring, curriculum, and mentoring resources. Those pages\nargue, and an argument needs metaphor, rhythm, and the long sentence that earns\nits length. Do not put a controlled vocabulary anywhere near them.\n\n### The nine rules\n\n1. **One idea per sentence.** If a sentence carries a condition and an action and\n   an exception, it is three sentences.\n2. **25 words maximum for explanation, 20 for an instruction.** Hard caps, not\n   targets. Section 10 already bans the 30-word sentence everywhere; this is the\n   tighter version for pages people work from.\n3. **One term, one meaning, one word.** Pick the word and reuse it. If the thing\n   is a charter, it is never also the constitution, the rulebook, or the policy\n   file. Variation for elegance costs the reader a lookup, and the glossary is\n   the place to record the choice.\n4. **Active voice, and name the actor.** \"The gate refuses the write,\" not \"the\n   write is refused.\"\n5. **Instructions in the imperative.** \"Run the seed script.\" Not \"the seed\n   script should then be run,\" and not \"you would run the seed script.\"\n6. **Condition before action.** \"If the queue is empty, stop the worker.\" The\n   reader who skips the first clause has skipped a guard, so the guard goes\n   first.\n7. **No noun stack longer than three words.** \"Agent run provenance record\n   schema\" becomes \"the schema for a provenance record,\" which is one word longer\n   and readable on the first pass.\n8. **A demonstrative carries its noun.** \"That gate,\" \"this budget,\" not a bare\n   \"that\" or \"this\" pointing back at a whole paragraph. This is the one place\n   where STE and the flow rules in section 6 pull against each other, and STE\n   wins on these pages: the tie has to be explicit anyway, so make it a phrase.\n9. **No idiom or metaphor inside a procedure.** Metaphor is allowed in the lede\n   and in the section that explains why the design is shaped this way. Inside a\n   numbered step it is a hazard, because the reader who does not share the idiom\n   has no way to guess.\n\n### What STE changes about the rest of this file\n\nThe caps compress cadence, so the section 9 targets shift on these pages:\n**median sentence 11 to 16 words, standard deviation above 6.** The tic budgets,\nthe banned list, the flow rules, the person targets, and section 7 are unchanged.\nA page written to a controlled vocabulary can still concede the counter-argument,\nstill state the cost first, and still admit the failed first attempt, and if it\ndoes not do those things it reads like a manual instead of like me.\n\n### What STE is not\n\nNot full ASD-STE100: there is no approved-word dictionary here, and no ban on\nthe words that specification bans for aerospace reasons. Not a ban on technical\nvocabulary, which is the opposite of the point, because the precise term is\nalways shorter than the paraphrase. Not applicable to the lede, which still gets\n90 words to state the whole thesis, in sentences of any shape it needs. The one\nrule the lede does not escape is the 30-word ban in section 10, because that ban\nis the Hemingway test rather than the controlled vocabulary, and a 35-word\nopening sentence is exactly where a reader decides whether to keep going.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Structure\n\nThree things hold for every piece:\n\n1. **Lede, 40 to 90 words.** The whole thesis, including the cost and the honest\n   limit. No throat-clearing.\n2. **Sentence case headings**, and a section exists only if the reader does\n   something differently after it.\n3. **The ending states a concrete next step.** \"Try this on one file this\n   week.\" An open question is the fallback for when no next step exists, as in\n   a philosophy post. Not a summary, and not an aphorism.\n\n**Resource pages and talks add:** a byline block reading exactly `Words by Ian\nJohnson, author of Harness Engineering · tacoda.dev` (no em dash, identical on\nevery page); a facts strip (counts, runtime, who it's for, what you need,\nlicense, middot-separated, same field order every time); and numbered sections.\n\n**Blog posts** are shorter and single-threaded, and skip the byline block, facts\nstrip, and section numbering. Everything else applies unchanged. A post is where\nthe ratio of thinking to apparatus is highest, which makes the first-person\nminimum matter more, not less. If a post has no \"I\" in it, it should have been a\nresource page.\n\n**Project documentation** keeps the maxims, the banned list, sentence case, the\ntic budgets, numbers instead of adjectives, and stated non-goals. Manner matters\nmost here, because a doc that is ambiguous has failed at its only job. What\nchanges:\n\n- Reader address stays high. Docs are instructions; the imperative is the default\n  mood.\n- The first-person minimum does not apply to reference material (API surface,\n  flags, config keys, error tables). It does apply to anything explaining a\n  choice: an ADR, a design note, a \"why it works this way\" section.\n- Cadence targets do not apply to reference tables. Narrative sections still hit\n  them.\n- Lead with the working example, then explain it.\n- State the failure mode and the escape hatch for every instruction that can\n  fail. A doc that only describes the happy path generates issues.\n- Say what the thing does not do, early.\n- No marketing register inside reference docs. Save that for the landing page.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Audience\n\nChange the argument, the evidence, and the entry point. Never the person, the\nwarmth, the banned list, the tic budgets, or the flow rules.\n\n| Audience | Entry point | Evidence they accept | Ask of them |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Practitioners** (most resources) | the annoying concrete problem | running code, counts, repro steps | try it on one file this week |\n| **Teams** | the seam where the work breaks: review, handoff, onboarding | a before and after on one shared workflow, a rule they can adopt Monday | one named change to how the team works |\n| **Leaders** (talks, org-level posts) | the decision on their table | second-order effects, timelines, headcount | one policy change, named |\n| **Business orgs** | the cost or risk already on their books | money, cycle time, exposure, what a competitor shipped | one budget or policy call |\n| **Early-career** | what they cannot yet see | worked example plus answer key | a 30-minute drill |\n| **General public** (philosophical posts) | the tension anyone can feel without the jargon | reasoning from stated premises, one grounding example | sit with the question, or change their default |\n\nThree rules survive every audience: concede the strong counter-argument first,\nstate the cost before the benefit, and stay concrete even when the subject is\nabstract.\n\nThe practitioner, team, leader, and business rows differ in scope, not altitude, and mixing them up is the common\nfailure. A team piece is about what several people do between them, so it lands\non a rule or a ritual rather than a technique. A leadership piece is about a\ndecision one person owns. A business piece is about money and risk, and it is\nthe row that most often drags in marketing voice, because the reader is used to\nbeing sold to. Section 3 still applies at full strength there: no \"faster\ndecisions\", no \"reduced friction\", no benefit stated without its mechanism or\nits number. Write to a CFO the way you would write to an engineer who happens\nnot to know the stack.\n\nThe general public row drops the jargon, never the rigor. Explaining a term is\nfine, and softening the claim to avoid explaining it is not.\n\n**Specialties nest inside practitioners.** A specialist gets a different entry\npoint, not a different voice: open by translating from what they already know,\ngive the mapping, then say where the mapping breaks. The `iii-*` pages do this\nfor OTP practitioners (supervision trees, restart intensity, mailboxes). The same\nmove works for Rails, Kubernetes, or data-engineering readers. Specialty\nvocabulary earns attention in the first paragraph and then gets out of the way.\n\n---\n\n## 14. Declaring a piece\n\nThree tags, so the check travels with the file and nobody has to remember:\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"voice-register\" content=\"informational\">   <!-- or argument, talk -->\n<meta name=\"voice-subject\"  content=\"third-party\">     <!-- only when true -->\n<meta name=\"voice-english\"  content=\"ste\">             <!-- technical pages -->\n```\n\nIn Markdown: `<!-- voice-register: argument -->`.\n\nDefault when absent is `argument` with unrestricted English, which is the\nstrictest register and the loosest vocabulary. Section 11 says which pages\ndeclare `ste`, and a technical walkthrough without the tag is a page nobody has\nchecked yet.\n\n---\n\n## 15. Checks\n\nThe checks that run on something I am writing to be read: a post, a chapter, a\nresource page, a talk, a landing page. Not a PR body, a commit message, a ticket,\nor a changelog entry, which section 6 of my CLAUDE.md already excludes and which\nthis chain would only make slower.\n\nThey run as a chain, in this order, each one narrower than the last:\n\n1. Draft.\n2. **`/voice-check <path>`. Authoritative.** Everything countable in this file:\n   the banned list, em dashes at zero, tic budgets, person, cadence, Hemingway,\n   concision, the substitution list. It rewrites nothing, so it is the one stage\n   that cannot introduce a defect. Its output is the constraint list the rest of\n   the chain runs under.\n3. **`/humanizer` for tone.** Machine cadence, uniform sentence length, tie-less\n   paragraphs, flat register. It may not reintroduce anything step 2 flagged and\n   may not reach for a phrase on the banned list.\n4. **`/slopbeth` for slop.** Generic AI tells, hollow connectives, restatement.\n   Bound by everything above, and additionally by whatever step 3 just fixed.\n5. **`/voice-check` again, as the gate.** Nothing new gets checked here. A\n   finding that reappears was put back by step 3 or 4, so fix it there.\n6. Ship.\n\n**Each filter is narrower than the one before it, and none may undo an earlier\none.** That property is the whole reason for the order. Steps 3 and 4 rewrite\nsentences, and a rewriter with no memory of what was already rejected will\ncheerfully hand back the em dash, the tidy triad, or the \"leverage\" that step 2\ncaught. So each stage carries the accumulated list forward as a hard constraint,\nand step 5 verifies that it did. The second voice-check is cheap, because it is\na script rather than a rewrite.\n\nTwo limits hold across steps 3 and 4. Neither may add a claim, a number, or a\nfirst-person experience the draft did not already contain. Neither may fix\nanything by chopping one idea into three sentences, which section 6 covers.\n\nAll three report signals; none decides whether the writing is good. Findings\nmarked `[advisory]` are the section 5 cases and usually mean leave it alone.\n\n**Over-editing prose that already works is a failure.** \"Leave this paragraph\nalone\" is a valid outcome, and a page whose numbers sit inside range but reads\nbadly is still a page to fix. Read it aloud; that catches what no script can.\n\n**Where this sits.** The `/write` skill is the SOP, eleven steps from topic\nto final draft, and this chain runs in its step 8. One process, described in two\nplaces because the chain also stands alone: run it directly when a piece is\nalready drafted and only needs the checks. Running it does not pull in the rest\nof the pipeline, and the pipeline does not restate what is written here.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Length\n\nA page nobody finishes taught nobody anything, so length is a correctness\nproperty rather than a matter of taste. **The ceiling is 30 minutes of reading,\nwhich is 4,500 words** of body prose, not counting code blocks and tables.\nTarget 3,000.\n\nBoth numbers come from measuring the published corpus. My resource pages have a\nmedian near 2,800 words, and the four that overran everything else ran 7,200 to\n20,800, which is where this section came from. I have not measured completion\nrates, so 4,500 is the ceiling I can defend and not a finding about attention.\n\n### What to cut first\n\nIn this order, because the early items cost the reader most and the argument\nleast:\n\n1. **The second example that proves the same point.** One worked example at full\n   depth teaches more than three at half depth. Keep the strongest, then name\n   the others in a sentence.\n2. **Low-level detail the reader will never type.** Exact flag spellings, whole\n   file listings, every field of a schema, the shape of a log line. Show the part\n   the argument turns on and link to the repo for the rest, because accuracy is\n   not negotiable and completeness is.\n3. **The recap section.** It restates what the last three sections said, and the\n   reader who needs it can scroll up.\n4. **Setup sentences.** Section 1 already bans them, and they survive anyway\n   because they feel like transitions. A transition ties two ideas; a setup\n   sentence announces one.\n5. **The exhaustive enumeration.** Seven guardrails where three carry the\n   design: say which three, note that four more exist.\n\n### Altitude\n\nPick one depth per page and hold it. A page that explains why a design is shaped\nthis way and then drops into per-line commentary has changed audiences\nmid-scroll, so the reader who came for the first thing quits at the second.\n\nWhen the detail is genuinely needed, give it its own URL: an appendix page, a\nrepo, a reference table. Splitting a 9,000-word page into a 3,000-word argument\nplus a linked reference is not a cut. It is two pages that each get read.\n\n### What cutting may not touch\n\nEverything in section 7 stays: numbers instead of adjectives, named costs,\nadmitted failure, the conceded counter-argument. Those sentences are what earn\nthe page, and a short page without them is a brochure. If a cut removes\nevidence, put the evidence back and cut a paragraph of connective prose instead.\n\nFlow survives too. Chopping ties to save words fails section 6 and reads worse\nthan the long version did, so cut whole units first, meaning an example, a\nsubsection, or a digression, and only then trim inside sentences.\n\nContents of /Users/tacoda/.tessl/RULES.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# Agent Rules\n\nThis file is updated when running `tessl install`. If a linked file is missing, make sure to run the command to download any missing plugins from the registry.\n\nContents of /Users/tacoda/.claude/rules/context7.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\nUse Context7 MCP to fetch current documentation whenever the user asks about a library, framework, SDK, API, CLI tool, or cloud service -- even well-known ones like React, Next.js, Prisma, Express, Tailwind, Django, or Spring Boot. This includes API syntax, configuration, version migration, library-specific debugging, setup instructions, and CLI tool usage. Use even when you think you know the answer -- your training data may not reflect recent changes. Prefer this over web search for library docs.\n\nDo not use for: refactoring, writing scripts from scratch, debugging business logic, code review, or general programming concepts.\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Always start with `resolve-library-id` using the library name and the user's question, unless the user provides an exact library ID in `/org/project` format\n2. Pick the best match (ID format: `/org/project`) by: exact name match, description relevance, code snippet count, source reputation (High/Medium preferred), and benchmark score (higher is better). If results don't look right, try alternate names or queries (e.g., \"next.js\" not \"nextjs\", or rephrase the question). Use version-specific IDs when the user mentions a version\n3. `query-docs` with the selected library ID and the user's full question (not single words)\n4. Answer using the fetched docs\n\nContents of /Users/tacoda/.claude/rules/session-hygiene.md (user's private global instructions for all projects):\n\n# Session Hygiene\n\nLong sessions drift. Context fills with stale exploration, abandoned attempts, and corrections that no longer apply. The agent gets less coherent, not more, the longer a session runs.\n\n## Reset triggers\n\n- **Corrected twice on the same thing** → `/clear` and re-prompt with the lesson built in. A third correction means the context is fighting you.\n- **Direction has changed** (different feature, different file, different intent) → `/clear`. Bringing yesterday's exploration into today's task is a slow leak.\n- **Session past ~45 minutes of active work** → `/compact`. The early turns are likely dead weight.\n\n## Separate clones for parallel work\n\nRun independent tasks in separate clones under `.clones/<branch-slug>/` (see `.claude/rules/branches.md`) with separate Claude sessions. Do not interleave two features in one session — the cross-talk costs more than the clone setup.\n\nFour concurrent clones is the hard cap (the port-slot count); two or three is the practical soft ceiling. Beyond that, attention divides faster than parallelism saves time.\n\n## What survives across sessions\n\n- Rules in `.claude/rules/` — reload on every session.\n- `CLAUDE.md`, `HARNESS.md`, `GLOSSARY.md` — same.\n- Feature docs in `.claude/features/` — read on demand.\n\nWhat does **not** survive: per-conversation discoveries. If a discovery is worth keeping, write it into a rule, a feature doc, or `GLOSSARY.md` *before* clearing.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Treating `/clear` as failure. It's a tool — use it.\n- Asking the agent to \"remember\" something across sessions without writing it down.\n- One mega-session that touches five features. Split it.\n\nContents of /Users/tacoda/tacoda/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# Document styles\n\nTen named house styles, extracted from tacoda.dev and its linked product sites,\nfulcorum.com, and pds. When building any page, deck, doc, or dashboard, **pick\none by name and read its file before writing markup.** Never blend two.\n\nIf the ask doesn't name a style, pick from the table and say which one you chose.\n\n| Name | Read | Feel | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **AURORA** | `styles/aurora.md` | Near-black, floating blurred blobs, frosted glass, animated gradient headlines | tacoda.dev main page and its sub-pages (`/about`, `/projects`, `/resources`). Portfolio and landing surfaces. |\n| **PLEXUS** | `styles/plexus.md` | Dark app shell, 280px sidebar, IBM Plex, mint accent, terminal/tree/pipeline diagrams | Documentation sites and product sites that double as docs. The Keystone look. |\n| **DAYLIGHT** | `styles/daylight.md` | Warm cream, system sans, purple actions, yellow section bars, dark code blocks | Open source landing pages, README-shaped marketing. The Open Refinery look. |\n| **LEDGER** | `styles/ledger.md` | Cool off-white, near-black ink, one slate blue, `key: value` rows on a 2-space indent rail, flat 1px rules | Config-driven tools where the file the reader edits *is* the product. Declarative CLIs, schema references. The Caml look. |\n| **SEAFOAM** | `styles/seafoam.md` | 760px column, all-mono, TUI grays + mint sweep, no chrome | CLI/TUI project pages. A README rendered as a web page. The Sigma look. |\n| **EMBER** | `styles/ember.md` | Charcoal + one warm amber accent, soft-cornered panels, no motion | Product/course marketing. The Fulcorum look. |\n| **LETTERPRESS** | `styles/letterpress.md` | Warm paper, serif body, **mono headings**, double rules, numbered rail, brick/gold/forest signals | Resources that get *read*: worked examples, standards, workshops, exercises. Long-form technical writing. |\n| **PODIUM** | `styles/podium.md` | Fixed 1280×720 stage, scaled to fit; sans display, serif ledes, amber/teal/red semantics | Resources tagged **Talk**. Self-contained browser slide decks. |\n| **CONTROLROOM** | `styles/controlroom.md` | All-mono 13px, cool near-black, hairlines, state carried by border color | Ops dashboards, job/queue boards, log and diff viewers, internal tools. The pds look. |\n| **NEBULA** | `styles/nebula.md` | Deep indigo space, nebula wash, glowing gradient-ringed cards, magenta→cyan | Full app shells — sidebar + content + right rail. High-energy product UI. |\n\n## How they split on tacoda.dev\n\n- Main page + `/about` + `/projects` + `/resources` → **AURORA** (one SPA, one style)\n- Projects → `/keystone` → **PLEXUS** · `/open-refinery` → **DAYLIGHT** · `/sigma` → **SEAFOAM**\n- Projects → *Also see* → fulcorum.com → **EMBER**\n- Resources → *documents, standard, workshop, exercise* → **LETTERPRESS**\n- Resources → *talks* (`/harness-reliable-governable/`, `/senior-2031/`) → **PODIUM**\n\nThe Projects page also lists the Ruby gems — Caml, Otto, Reins:\n`/caml` → **LEDGER** · Otto → **LETTERPRESS** · Reins → **NEBULA**\n\n## Picking between the dark technical styles\n\n- **SEAFOAM** — one page of prose about a CLI. Under ~2KB of CSS.\n- **PLEXUS** — the same audience once it needs navigation and diagrams.\n- **CONTROLROOM** — not reading, *monitoring*. Hundreds of rows, state at a glance.\n- **NEBULA** — a product surface people choose to look at.\n\n## Picking between the light styles\n\n- **DAYLIGHT** — warm cream, cards, purple actions. Marketing that wants to feel friendly.\n- **LETTERPRESS** — warm paper, serif body, semantic brick/gold/forest. Long-form reading.\n- **LEDGER** — cool off-white, `key: value` rows, no cards. When the config file *is* the product.\n\n## Shared rules\n\n- LETTERPRESS, PODIUM, DAYLIGHT, and LEDGER ship light *and* dark. LETTERPRESS,\n  PODIUM, and LEDGER must honor both `prefers-color-scheme` and a\n  `:root[data-theme]` override, with the explicit toggle winning; DAYLIGHT\n  follows the media query only. DAYLIGHT, LETTERPRESS, and LEDGER are\n  light-first. AURORA, PLEXUS, SEAFOAM, EMBER, CONTROLROOM, and NEBULA are\n  dark-only.\n- All animation stops under `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)`.\n- Tokens go in `:root` as CSS custom properties. AURORA is the one exception —\n  it uses Tailwind CDN with an inline `tailwind.config`.\n- Each page is a single self-contained HTML file. No build step, no bundler.\n\nContents of /Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion/CLAUDE.md (project instructions, checked into the codebase):\n\n# CLAUDE.md\n\n## The project\n\nBastion is a server and its clients. [PLAN.md](PLAN.md) holds the decisions\n(cited in code as `PLAN.md D<n>`) and the phases; read §3 and §5 before a\nchange.\n\n- `crates/bastion-core`: the factory's rules, pure. It may depend only on\n  `serde` and `sha2`.\n- `crates/bastion-protocol`: the API's types and routes. Only `serde`.\n- `crates/bastion-server`: the HTTP API, events, the factory adapter, the\n  store, the gate, the audit, the stack. Only `src/engine/` imports `iii-sdk`.\n- `crates/bastion-client`: what every interface uses to reach a server: the\n  server list, the local server's start, the API calls, the event stream.\n  Never `bastion-core` or `iii-sdk`.\n- `crates/bastion-cli`: the `bastion` command line, on `bastion-client`.\n- `crates/bastion-desktop`: the iced client. It reaches a server only through\n  `bastion-client`; never through `bastion-core` or `iii-sdk`.\n- `workers/`: Bastion's own iii workers.\n\n## Before a commit\n\nRun `pre_commit_code_health_safeguard` and commit only when it passes. After\nchanging code, run `code_health_review` on the changed files, and if Code\nHealth drops, fix it and confirm with `code_health_score`.\n\n## Before a push\n\nRun every CI step, not only the tests:\n\n```sh\ncargo fmt --all -- --check\nscripts/check-seams.sh\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo test --workspace\n```\n\nContents of /Users/tacoda/.claude/projects/-Users-tacoda-tacoda-bastion/memory/MEMORY.md (user's auto-memory, persists across conversations):\n\n# Memory index\n\n- [Bastion dev workflow](bastion-dev-workflow.md) — phase-by-phase, browser-verified loop; frontend runs locally, backend in Docker\n- [Bastion deploy](bastion-deploy.md) — Heroku app DESTROYED 2026-07-16; single-role RLS + PG/Redis TLS gotchas kept for redeploy\n- [Bastion governance package](bastion-governance-package.md) — sibling repo ~/tacoda/tacoda/bastion-governance; @bastion/governance npm CLI+SDK; control plane governs not invokes\n- [Prefer momentum over asking](prefer-momentum-over-asking.md) — proceed on stated assumptions; reserve questions for costly forks; keep current Policy model\n- [Push over pull](push-over-pull.md) — iii.observe over polling triggers (Bastion F25)\n- [Bastion Rust app](bastion-rust-app.md) — native Rust factory on iii, job-first UI like GitHub Actions, wipp pipeline, no editor; PLAN.md governs\n- [Bastion server terminology](bastion-server-terminology.md) — say \"Bastion server\", never target/profile; servers hold workspaces (one per repo); job picks server\n- [Multi-user and hosting are open](bastion-multi-user-hosting-open.md) — not non-goals despite PLAN.md §2; never claim \"not hosted\" or \"one owner\"\n- [Local needs no credentials](bastion-local-needs-no-credentials.md) — local Bastion server: automatic token only; tokens and people are for remote servers\n- [Run every CI step](bastion-run-every-ci-step.md) — seams and both -D warnings clippies before each push; core may not use serde_json\n- [Factory, not orchestrator](bastion-orchestrator-terminology.md) — rebrand to orchestrator/control plane rolled back 2026-11-06; keep factory words\n- [No full process args](no-full-process-args.md) — never pgrep -fl/ps aux; iii VM boot lines carry host secrets\n- [Only Dark runs unsupervised](only-dark-runs-unsupervised.md) — lights-on waits for a person; Claude may act for the user on test jobs, saying each action\n</system-reminder>"}],"renderedRole":"user","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
