{"parentUuid":"cbb91634-5220-4d5c-b1d5-a98f851bc27e","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01HT4ST2jpPrbbsJ6FuXzGAA","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"if [ -n \"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_SPOOL_DIR:-}\" ] && [ -x /bin/zsh ]; then exec /bin/zsh -fc 'LC_ALL=C␤export CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-$PPID}␤export CMUX_AGENT_HOOK_INVOKER_PID=$PPID␤cmux_fallback() { { print -rn -- \"$cmux_p\"; /bin/cat; } | /bin/sh -c \"$1\" >&3; exit $?; }␤zmodload zsh/system zsh/datetime zsh/files 2>/dev/null || exec /bin/sh -c \"$1\" >&3␤cmux_p= cmux_c= cmux_e=0␤while (( ${#cmux_p} < 262144 )); do sysread -i 0 -s 65536 cmux_c || { cmux_e=$?; break; }; cmux_p+=$cmux_c; done␤[[ -n ${CMUX_SURFACE_ID:-} && ${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED:-} != 1 ]] || { (( cmux_e == 5 )) || /bin/cat >/dev/null; print -r -- '\\''{}'\\'' >&3; exit 0; }␤cmux_d=$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_SPOOL_DIR␤(( cmux_e == 5 )) && [[ -f $cmux_d/keys ]] || cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤cmux_r=\"cmux-agent-hook-v1\"$'\\''\\n'\\''\"claude\"$'\\''\\n'\\''\"$2\"$'\\''\\n'\\''␤for cmux_k in ${(f)\"$(<$cmux_d/keys)\"}; do (( ${+parameters[$cmux_k]} )) && cmux_r+=\"$cmux_k=${(P)cmux_k}\"$'\\''\\0'\\''; done␤cmux_r+=$'\\''\\0'\\''\"$cmux_p\"␤(( ${#cmux_r} <= 524288 )) || cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤umask 077␤cmux_n=$cmux_d/$epochtime[1].$epochtime[2]-$$␤{ print -rn -- \"$cmux_r\" >| $cmux_n.tmp && mv -f -- $cmux_n.tmp $cmux_n.rec; } 2>/dev/null || { rm -f -- $cmux_n.tmp 2>/dev/null; cmux_fallback \"$1\"; }␤if zsystem flock -t 0 -r -f cmux_l $cmux_d/forwarder.lock 2>/dev/null; then␤  zsystem flock -u $cmux_l␤  rm -- $cmux_n.rec 2>/dev/null && cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤fi␤print -r -- '\\''{}'\\'' >&3' cmux-hook 'cmux_cli=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_CMUX_BIN:-${CMUX_BUNDLED_CLI_PATH:-}}\"; if [ -z \"$cmux_cli\" ] || [ ! -x \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then cmux_cli=\"$(command -v cmux 2>/dev/null || true)\"; fi; agent_pid=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-${PPID:-}}\"; if [ -n \"$CMUX_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; fi' pre-tool-use 3>&1 1>&2; else cmux_cli=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_CMUX_BIN:-${CMUX_BUNDLED_CLI_PATH:-}}\"; if [ -z \"$cmux_cli\" ] || [ ! -x \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then cmux_cli=\"$(command -v cmux 2>/dev/null || true)\"; fi; agent_pid=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-${PPID:-}}\"; if [ -n \"$CMUX_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; fi; fi","durationMs":59},"type":"attachment","uuid":"248825e3-5625-49c0-acb2-dcbe8dc7bd07","timestamp":"2026-11-11T21:44:03.075Z","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"cbb91634-5220-4d5c-b1d5-a98f851bc27e","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"1d9ef2db-03d5-4a58-b720-e211c5a548e5","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HT4ST2jpPrbbsJ6FuXzGAA","type":"tool_result","content":"# Bastion decisions\n\nBastion is a software factory you can govern. A request goes in, agents work\nit through a workflow of steps a person can watch and gate, and a reviewed\nchange comes out. Bastion is a server and its clients: `bastion-server`\nruns the factory on the iii engine, and clients such as the iced desktop app\nreach it over HTTP.\n\nThis file holds the goals and the decisions the code cites as\n`DECISIONS.md D<n>`. The plans that built Bastion, phase by phase, are in\ngit history.\n\n## Goals and non-goals\n\n**Goals:**\n\n1. A person sees what every job does and decides what it may do.\n2. A change Bastion lands passed the checks the owner asked for, with the\n   evidence on the job.\n3. A restart, an outage or a lost VM never loses a record or corrupts a job.\n4. Any client and any number of servers, through one API.\n\n**Non-goals for now:** a web client, managed hosting, a plugin system for\ncode-defined guards (rules stay data, per D20 and D26), and a sign-in with a\nClaude subscription (D36). Multi-user servers and hosting stay open\nquestions (below), not non-goals.\n\n## Decisions\n\nEach decision keeps the number the code cites. Numbers not listed were\nretired with the old plan.\n\n| # | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| D1 | Rust for all Bastion code. Another language only where no Rust path exists, named here with its reason: `.pi/extensions/entire/index.ts` is generated by the Entire tool and is not Bastion code. |\n| D4 | One dark theme, purple as the main color. Its tokens live in `crates/bastion-desktop/src/theme.rs`. |\n| D6 | Every Bastion worker is a Rust binary on `iii-sdk` 0.25.0, started by compose through `scripts.run`. |\n| D7 | A stock iii worker comes first. Bastion writes a worker only for what no pinned worker does. |\n| D8 | Records live in the `database` worker as typed columns. Each row carries a `rev`, and a stale write is refused. |\n| D10 | Updates are pushed, never polled: the server observes the engine, and clients follow the server's events (D29). |\n| D13 | The local server starts the stack (`iii compose`) and attaches to one that already runs. The stack goes down when the server stops. A client attaches to the local server or starts it, and quitting a client leaves both running. |\n| D14 | Every agent turn runs in an `iii-sandbox` VM on its job's worktree. No credential enters the VM; pushes and pull requests happen on the host. |\n| D16 | A note on a step goes to the agent word for word as its next brief. A fix loop stops after a set count, or sooner when a refusal repeats word for word. |\n| D17 | Every job runs a workflow. A free-form task is a short workflow, not a second mode. |\n| D18 | The factory's rules live in `bastion-core`: pure functions from an event and a state to a new state and effects, with no I/O and no `async`. |\n| D19 | The seams are Cargo dependencies, and `scripts/check-seams.sh` fails CI when one is crossed. Only the engine adapter imports `iii-sdk`. Clients depend on `bastion-protocol` and the client library only. |\n| D20 | Every constraint and capability is enforced in a hook, and its rung is data, so an eval can name what changed. |\n| D21 | The audit record is a hash chain with one writer. Each entry carries an HMAC-SHA256 of its fields and the entry before it, keyed with a secret outside the record. |\n| D22 | An eval compares two harness configurations on the same requests from the same commit, and reports the difference with the decisions behind it. |\n| D23 | Forge, tracker, CI, model providers and notifications are adapters behind seams, chosen in Settings. |\n| D25 | Every rule belongs to one layer: the charter (the repository's own), the harness (an agent's calls), or the factory (the workflow). |\n| D26 | Rules, grants and settings are data in the server's database, versioned, edited on the pages; first-run defaults come from `seed/governance.sql`. |\n| D27 | The server is the source of truth and holds all business logic. A client shows state and sends a person's actions, nothing more. |\n| D28 | The API is HTTP with JSON, one route per action on one resource, typed in `bastion-protocol`, with a bearer token on every request. |\n| D29 | Push is server-sent events on `GET /events`, with ids. A client that reconnects with `Last-Event-ID` gets what it missed, or a `resync`. |\n| D30 | A client keeps a list of servers, each a name, a URL and a token in the OS keychain. `local` is the server on this machine. A job runs on one server. |\n| D31 | A server has people with roles: an admin manages it, a member submits and acts on their own jobs, a viewer reads. Tokens are issued, shown once and kept as a hash. Every record names its actor. |\n| D32 | A server listens on loopback unless it has a TLS certificate and key. |\n| D33 | Each step is lights-on or dark. A workflow's level follows from its steps. No step waits on a timer. Every decision is logged, whatever the dial. |\n| D34 | Model provider keys live in the stack's vault, read only by `llm-router`, never in the environment and never returned. |\n| D35 | A workflow is a graph of steps a person edits on a canvas. Steps pass declared results to the steps linked after them. |\n| D36 | Claude takes an API key only. No Claude subscription sign-in. |\n| D37 | The desktop client is a native Rust app on `iced`. It holds no factory logic and reaches a server only through the client library. |\n| D38 | Every client goes through `crates/bastion-client`: the server list, typed calls, and the event stream. A new interface adds pages, not a second way to talk to a server. |\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Dark to merge.** ghola never lets a factory merge unattended; Bastion's\n  dark dial can run to merge. Should merge always need a person, or a machine\n  verdict, such as the `verify.yaml` contract?\n- **Configuration in git.** ghola keeps configuration in files under review;\n  Bastion keeps it in the database with versions (D26). Should rules also be\n  importable as files, such as team rule packs shared by servers?\n- **Multi-user hosting.** A shared server with people and TLS works today.\n  Is hosted Bastion a goal?\n- **Unused stack workers.** C4 lists them; some may be the harness's\n  dependencies.\n# Bastion plan: a workflow is its steps\n\n<!-- voice-register: informational -->\n<!-- voice-english: ste -->\n\nA job runs its workflow's steps in order, and nothing else. A step is one of\nfive kinds, and each kind has one text to edit. Bastion seeds the common\nworkflows with that text filled in, and the owner can change all of it. The\nstage concept goes away: no step has behavior that its text does not say.\nThe cost is the safety the built-in stages gave. Landing, pull requests,\nmerges, quality gates and acceptance tests become text the owner can read\nand change, not code Bastion enforces.\n\nDecisions are in [DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md) and cited as `DECISIONS.md\nD<n>`. D39 to D42 below are new; the stage decisions they replace say so.\n\n## The decisions\n\n- **D39. A workflow is a series of steps.** A step is a prompt, an agent\n  (`.claude/agents/<name>.md`), a skill (`.claude/skills/<name>`), a command\n  (`.claude/commands/<name>.md`), or a shell script. Each has a dial (a sun\n  or a moon), the results it gives, and the results linked into it. A job\n  ends when its last step passes. This replaces the stages in D18 and D35.\n- **D40. A shell step runs in the job's VM.** By default the VM has no\n  network. A step can ask for the network and name the vault's secrets it\n  gets as environment variables, such as `GH_TOKEN`. Code from the job's\n  branch never runs on the host.\n- **D41. A job ends with its branch ready, and the workspace says what\n  happens next.** The VM's commits come back to the job's branch on the host\n  after every step. When the last step passes, a workspace set to merge\n  merges the branch into its base on the host; a workspace set to leave it\n  keeps the branch. A pull request is a shell step that pushes and runs\n  `gh pr create`.\n- **D42. Governance is per step.** A step either may change files or only\n  reads. The capability ladder applies to a step's kind, and a step can\n  narrow it. Dials, notices and evals name steps, not stages.\n\n## Phases\n\nEach phase passes CI, is checked live, and is committed before the next.\n(This step only reads: it may not write a file.) sandbox::fs::write\n/work/repo/NOTES.md\". The settings' dials per stage and the evals' arms\nper stage move to S4, as they reach only the stage code it deletes.\n\n### S4. The stage code goes\n\n- Delete the built-in stages and the pipelines.\n- Delete the features that read a stage's output. These are scope splits,\n  discover questions, the acceptance tests, UAT sign-off, learn, escapes,\n  and the CI and deploy watches.\n- A migration moves stored jobs and workflows to steps.\n- DECISIONS.md marks each replaced decision, and the README says what\n  Bastion is now.\n- Exit: no `Stage` in the code but the five kinds, and every CI step passes.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **What learn and escapes become.** Both read a landed job's history. They\n  could come back as a seeded prompt step that reads the job's record.\n</content>\n</invoke>","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"5a55cd66-8233-4a5e-a5b8-2f4b47b9c3a3","timestamp":"2026-11-11T21:44:03.787Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Bastion decisions\n\nBastion is a software factory you can govern. A request goes in, agents work\nit through a workflow of steps a person can watch and gate, and a reviewed\nchange comes out. Bastion is a server and its clients: `bastion-server`\nruns the factory on the iii engine, and clients such as the iced desktop app\nreach it over HTTP.\n\nThis file holds the goals and the decisions the code cites as\n`DECISIONS.md D<n>`. The plans that built Bastion, phase by phase, are in\ngit history.\n\n## Goals and non-goals\n\n**Goals:**\n\n1. A person sees what every job does and decides what it may do.\n2. A change Bastion lands passed the checks the owner asked for, with the\n   evidence on the job.\n3. A restart, an outage or a lost VM never loses a record or corrupts a job.\n4. Any client and any number of servers, through one API.\n\n**Non-goals for now:** a web client, managed hosting, a plugin system for\ncode-defined guards (rules stay data, per D20 and D26), and a sign-in with a\nClaude subscription (D36). Multi-user servers and hosting stay open\nquestions (below), not non-goals.\n\n## Decisions\n\nEach decision keeps the number the code cites. Numbers not listed were\nretired with the old plan.\n\n| # | Decision |\n|---|---|\n| D1 | Rust for all Bastion code. Another language only where no Rust path exists, named here with its reason: `.pi/extensions/entire/index.ts` is generated by the Entire tool and is not Bastion code. |\n| D4 | One dark theme, purple as the main color. Its tokens live in `crates/bastion-desktop/src/theme.rs`. |\n| D6 | Every Bastion worker is a Rust binary on `iii-sdk` 0.25.0, started by compose through `scripts.run`. |\n| D7 | A stock iii worker comes first. Bastion writes a worker only for what no pinned worker does. |\n| D8 | Records live in the `database` worker as typed columns. Each row carries a `rev`, and a stale write is refused. |\n| D10 | Updates are pushed, never polled: the server observes the engine, and clients follow the server's events (D29). |\n| D13 | The local server starts the stack (`iii compose`) and attaches to one that already runs. The stack goes down when the server stops. A client attaches to the local server or starts it, and quitting a client leaves both running. |\n| D14 | Every agent turn runs in an `iii-sandbox` VM on its job's worktree. No credential enters the VM; pushes and pull requests happen on the host. |\n| D16 | A note on a step goes to the agent word for word as its next brief. A fix loop stops after a set count, or sooner when a refusal repeats word for word. |\n| D17 | Every job runs a workflow. A free-form task is a short workflow, not a second mode. |\n| D18 | The factory's rules live in `bastion-core`: pure functions from an event and a state to a new state and effects, with no I/O and no `async`. |\n| D19 | The seams are Cargo dependencies, and `scripts/check-seams.sh` fails CI when one is crossed. Only the engine adapter imports `iii-sdk`. Clients depend on `bastion-protocol` and the client library only. |\n| D20 | Every constraint and capability is enforced in a hook, and its rung is data, so an eval can name what changed. |\n| D21 | The audit record is a hash chain with one writer. Each entry carries an HMAC-SHA256 of its fields and the entry before it, keyed with a secret outside the record. |\n| D22 | An eval compares two harness configurations on the same requests from the same commit, and reports the difference with the decisions behind it. |\n| D23 | Forge, tracker, CI, model providers and notifications are adapters behind seams, chosen in Settings. |\n| D25 | Every rule belongs to one layer: the charter (the repository's own), the harness (an agent's calls), or the factory (the workflow). |\n| D26 | Rules, grants and settings are data in the server's database, versioned, edited on the pages; first-run defaults come from `seed/governance.sql`. |\n| D27 | The server is the source of truth and holds all business logic. A client shows state and sends a person's actions, nothing more. |\n| D28 | The API is HTTP with JSON, one route per action on one resource, typed in `bastion-protocol`, with a bearer token on every request. |\n| D29 | Push is server-sent events on `GET /events`, with ids. A client that reconnects with `Last-Event-ID` gets what it missed, or a `resync`. |\n| D30 | A client keeps a list of servers, each a name, a URL and a token in the OS keychain. `local` is the server on this machine. A job runs on one server. |\n| D31 | A server has people with roles: an admin manages it, a member submits and acts on their own jobs, a viewer reads. Tokens are issued, shown once and kept as a hash. Every record names its actor. |\n| D32 | A server listens on loopback unless it has a TLS certificate and key. |\n| D33 | Each step is lights-on or dark. A workflow's level follows from its steps. No step waits on a timer. Every decision is logged, whatever the dial. |\n| D34 | Model provider keys live in the stack's vault, read only by `llm-router`, never in the environment and never returned. |\n| D35 | A workflow is a graph of steps a person edits on a canvas. Steps pass declared results to the steps linked after them. |\n| D36 | Claude takes an API key only. No Claude subscription sign-in. |\n| D37 | The desktop client is a native Rust app on `iced`. It holds no factory logic and reaches a server only through the client library. |\n| D38 | Every client goes through `crates/bastion-client`: the server list, typed calls, and the event stream. A new interface adds pages, not a second way to talk to a server. |\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **Dark to merge.** ghola never lets a factory merge unattended; Bastion's\n  dark dial can run to merge. Should merge always need a person, or a machine\n  verdict, such as the `verify.yaml` contract?\n- **Configuration in git.** ghola keeps configuration in files under review;\n  Bastion keeps it in the database with versions (D26). Should rules also be\n  importable as files, such as team rule packs shared by servers?\n- **Multi-user hosting.** A shared server with people and TLS works today.\n  Is hosted Bastion a goal?\n- **Unused stack workers.** C4 lists them; some may be the harness's\n  dependencies.\n# Bastion plan: a workflow is its steps\n\n<!-- voice-register: informational -->\n<!-- voice-english: ste -->\n\nA job runs its workflow's steps in order, and nothing else. A step is one of\nfive kinds, and each kind has one text to edit. Bastion seeds the common\nworkflows with that text filled in, and the owner can change all of it. The\nstage concept goes away: no step has behavior that its text does not say.\nThe cost is the safety the built-in stages gave. Landing, pull requests,\nmerges, quality gates and acceptance tests become text the owner can read\nand change, not code Bastion enforces.\n\nDecisions are in [DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md) and cited as `DECISIONS.md\nD<n>`. D39 to D42 below are new; the stage decisions they replace say so.\n\n## The decisions\n\n- **D39. A workflow is a series of steps.** A step is a prompt, an agent\n  (`.claude/agents/<name>.md`), a skill (`.claude/skills/<name>`), a command\n  (`.claude/commands/<name>.md`), or a shell script. Each has a dial (a sun\n  or a moon), the results it gives, and the results linked into it. A job\n  ends when its last step passes. This replaces the stages in D18 and D35.\n- **D40. A shell step runs in the job's VM.** By default the VM has no\n  network. A step can ask for the network and name the vault's secrets it\n  gets as environment variables, such as `GH_TOKEN`. Code from the job's\n  branch never runs on the host.\n- **D41. A job ends with its branch ready, and the workspace says what\n  happens next.** The VM's commits come back to the job's branch on the host\n  after every step. When the last step passes, a workspace set to merge\n  merges the branch into its base on the host; a workspace set to leave it\n  keeps the branch. A pull request is a shell step that pushes and runs\n  `gh pr create`.\n- **D42. Governance is per step.** A step either may change files or only\n  reads. The capability ladder applies to a step's kind, and a step can\n  narrow it. Dials, notices and evals name steps, not stages.\n\n## Phases\n\nEach phase passes CI, is checked live, and is committed before the next.\n(This step only reads: it may not write a file.) sandbox::fs::write\n/work/repo/NOTES.md\". The settings' dials per stage and the evals' arms\nper stage move to S4, as they reach only the stage code it deletes.\n\n### S4. The stage code goes\n\n- Delete the built-in stages and the pipelines.\n- Delete the features that read a stage's output. These are scope splits,\n  discover questions, the acceptance tests, UAT sign-off, learn, escapes,\n  and the CI and deploy watches.\n- A migration moves stored jobs and workflows to steps.\n- DECISIONS.md marks each replaced decision, and the README says what\n  Bastion is now.\n- Exit: no `Stage` in the code but the five kinds, and every CI step passes.\n\n## Open questions\n\n- **What learn and escapes become.** Both read a landed job's history. They\n  could come back as a seeded prompt step that reads the job's record.\n</content>\n</invoke>","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"edc0ce8f-b496-450a-b24a-08854bba42b6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","root":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":11,"modified":54,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":3},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"cbb91634-5220-4d5c-b1d5-a98f851bc27e","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
