{"parentUuid":"3c895744-0007-416e-bef9-61c8b4a810c7","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:Bash","toolUseID":"toolu_01FTpNb9h9vUzpyMJZbueBSB","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"if [ -n \"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_SPOOL_DIR:-}\" ] && [ -x /bin/zsh ]; then exec /bin/zsh -fc 'LC_ALL=C␤export CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-$PPID}␤export CMUX_AGENT_HOOK_INVOKER_PID=$PPID␤cmux_fallback() { { print -rn -- \"$cmux_p\"; /bin/cat; } | /bin/sh -c \"$1\" >&3; exit $?; }␤zmodload zsh/system zsh/datetime zsh/files 2>/dev/null || exec /bin/sh -c \"$1\" >&3␤cmux_p= cmux_c= cmux_e=0␤while (( ${#cmux_p} < 262144 )); do sysread -i 0 -s 65536 cmux_c || { cmux_e=$?; break; }; cmux_p+=$cmux_c; done␤[[ -n ${CMUX_SURFACE_ID:-} && ${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED:-} != 1 ]] || { (( cmux_e == 5 )) || /bin/cat >/dev/null; print -r -- '\\''{}'\\'' >&3; exit 0; }␤cmux_d=$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_SPOOL_DIR␤(( cmux_e == 5 )) && [[ -f $cmux_d/keys ]] || cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤cmux_r=\"cmux-agent-hook-v1\"$'\\''\\n'\\''\"claude\"$'\\''\\n'\\''\"$2\"$'\\''\\n'\\''␤for cmux_k in ${(f)\"$(<$cmux_d/keys)\"}; do (( ${+parameters[$cmux_k]} )) && cmux_r+=\"$cmux_k=${(P)cmux_k}\"$'\\''\\0'\\''; done␤cmux_r+=$'\\''\\0'\\''\"$cmux_p\"␤(( ${#cmux_r} <= 524288 )) || cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤umask 077␤cmux_n=$cmux_d/$epochtime[1].$epochtime[2]-$$␤{ print -rn -- \"$cmux_r\" >| $cmux_n.tmp && mv -f -- $cmux_n.tmp $cmux_n.rec; } 2>/dev/null || { rm -f -- $cmux_n.tmp 2>/dev/null; cmux_fallback \"$1\"; }␤if zsystem flock -t 0 -r -f cmux_l $cmux_d/forwarder.lock 2>/dev/null; then␤  zsystem flock -u $cmux_l␤  rm -- $cmux_n.rec 2>/dev/null && cmux_fallback \"$1\"␤fi␤print -r -- '\\''{}'\\'' >&3' cmux-hook 'cmux_cli=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_CMUX_BIN:-${CMUX_BUNDLED_CLI_PATH:-}}\"; if [ -z \"$cmux_cli\" ] || [ ! -x \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then cmux_cli=\"$(command -v cmux 2>/dev/null || true)\"; fi; agent_pid=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-${PPID:-}}\"; if [ -n \"$CMUX_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '\\''{}'\\''; fi' pre-tool-use 3>&1 1>&2; else cmux_cli=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOK_CMUX_BIN:-${CMUX_BUNDLED_CLI_PATH:-}}\"; if [ -z \"$cmux_cli\" ] || [ ! -x \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then cmux_cli=\"$(command -v cmux 2>/dev/null || true)\"; fi; agent_pid=\"${CMUX_CLAUDE_PID:-${PPID:-}}\"; if [ -n \"$CMUX_SURFACE_ID\" ] && [ \"$CMUX_CLAUDE_HOOKS_DISABLED\" != \"1\" ] && [ -n \"$cmux_cli\" ]; then if [ -n \"${CMUX_SOCKET_PATH:-}\" ]; then CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" --socket \"$CMUX_SOCKET_PATH\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; else CMUX_CLAUDE_PID=\"$agent_pid\" CMUXTERM_CLI_RESPONSE_TIMEOUT_SEC=0.5 \"$cmux_cli\" hooks enqueue claude pre-tool-use 2>/dev/null || { cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; }; fi; else cat >/dev/null; echo '{}'; fi; fi","durationMs":58},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4f5058b6-a0e5-4f3d-b1f7-9731c9bf9939","timestamp":"2026-11-11T21:44:08.894Z","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
{"parentUuid":"3c895744-0007-416e-bef9-61c8b4a810c7","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"1d9ef2db-03d5-4a58-b720-e211c5a548e5","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FTpNb9h9vUzpyMJZbueBSB","type":"tool_result","content":"# Bastion\n\nBastion is a software factory you can govern. A request goes in, agents work\nit through a workflow, and a reviewed change comes out. A workflow is a graph\nof steps (Bastion's own stages, skills, prompts and commands) that you see on\na canvas, and each step waits for your approval or runs on its own, as you set\nit. Bastion is a server and its clients: `bastion-server` runs the factory on\nthe [iii](https://iii.dev) engine, and the desktop app (on\n[iced](https://iced.rs)) and the `bastion` command line talk to it over HTTP.\nIt's early. [VISION.md](VISION.md) says where it is going, and\n[DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md) holds the goals and the decisions behind it.\n\n## Install\n\nOn an Apple Silicon Mac or on Linux, install the server and the CLI, and add\n`--desktop` for the app:\n\n```sh\ncurl -fsSL https://tacoda.github.io/bastion/install.sh | sh -s -- --desktop\n```\n\nOn a Mac, Homebrew works too: `brew install --cask tacoda/bastion/bastion`.\nThe installer checks each download against the release's signed checksums,\nand `bastion update` moves you to the newest release. On a Linux VM, add\n`--service` to run the server as a systemd user service.\n\n## Run it from source\n\n```sh\ncargo build -p bastion-server\ncargo run -p bastion-desktop\n```\n\nThe client starts a local server if none runs on port 49190, and the server\nstarts the stack in `worker-compose.yaml`. Go to **Engine** to see Doctor and\nthe stack, then **Jobs** and **New request** to start work.\n\n## What it is not\n\nNot an editor: the only code you see in Bastion is the diff a job proposes.\nNot a web app. Not Windows, because sandbox VMs need Apple Silicon or Linux\nwith `/dev/kvm`. A server listens on loopback unless you give it a TLS\ncertificate, and then several people can share it, each with a token the\nserver issued them.\n\n## What you need\n\n- Rust, from `rust-toolchain.toml` (rustup picks it up).\n- `iii` 0.25.0 on your `PATH`.\n- A model provider key, set in **Settings**, which keeps it in the stack's\n  vault. A key in the environment works too.\n- `gh`, signed in, for pushes and pull requests. Landing locally works\n  without it.\n\nIf something is missing, Doctor on the Engine page names it and says how to\nfix it. A failed required check stops the start.\n\n## How it fits together\n\n| Part | Where | Does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core | `crates/bastion-core` | The factory's rules, as pure functions. |\n| Protocol | `crates/bastion-protocol` | The API's types and routes, shared by the server and its clients. |\n| Server | `crates/bastion-server` | The HTTP API and its events, the factory, the store, the gate, the audit and the stack. |\n| Engine adapter | `crates/bastion-server/src/engine/` | The only server code that imports `iii-sdk`. |\n| Client library | `crates/bastion-client` | What every client uses to reach a server: the server list, the local server's start, the calls and the event stream. |\n| Desktop client | `crates/bastion-desktop` | The pages, on iced. |\n| Command line | `crates/bastion-cli` | `bastion`: list and follow jobs, submit a request, approve, send back, stop, update. |\n| Workers | `workers/` | Bastion's own iii workers. |\n\n`scripts/check-seams.sh` fails the build when code crosses a seam (DECISIONS.md\nD19).\n\nWhat the engine was found to do, and on which versions, is in\n`docs/engine-findings.md`.\n\n## Checks\n\n```sh\ncargo fmt --all -- --check\nscripts/check-seams.sh\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo test --workspace\n```\n\nCI runs the same on macOS and Linux. The live checks need a running stack:\n`cargo test -p bastion-server -- --ignored --nocapture`.\nEach phase passes CI, is checked live, and is committed before the next.\n\n### S1. Steps run on their own\n\n- Shell steps ask for the network and name secrets (D40); the factory\n  makes the VM to match.\n- A job whose workflow has only the five kinds runs to its end, then\n  finishes by the workspace's setting (D41). The landing check that wants a\n  land stage accepts such a workflow.\n- The workspace gets its finish setting: merge, or leave the branch.\n- Exit, live: a workflow of a planning prompt, a building prompt, a test\n  shell step and a network shell step that opens a pull request on\n  `ianparo/bastion-demo`.\n\nDone 2026-11-11. On `demo-gh`, a workflow of a planning prompt, a\nbuilding prompt, a test shell step and a network shell step ran to its\nend. Plan gave its `plan` result, build committed, the test step failed\non the repository's own broken test and was shipped anyway, and the\nnetwork step pushed with `GH_TOKEN` from the vault and opened PR #2 on\n`ianparo/bastion-demo`. The job then finished, and the workspace left its\nbranch. The run found that an approved finish left the job waiting; that\nis fixed. The test workflow's own script misread GitHub's reply.\n\n### S2. The seeded workflows are steps\n\n- Each seeded workflow becomes the five kinds, with its text filled in.\n- The plan and build prompts start from today's briefs, and review is a\n  prompt. The pull request, the CI wait and the merge are `gh` shell scripts.\n- \"Runs as\" lists only the five kinds.\n- Exit, live: the seeded short workflow lands a change on `demo-local`, and\n  the seeded pull request workflow opens and merges a pull request.\n\nDone 2026-11-11. Short, Pull request, Full and the full workflow without\nacceptance tests ship as steps, saved at startup while no workflow has\ntheir id; the eleven older seeded workflows turn into steps while their\nfirst version is unchanged. Live: Short landed `negate(x)` on\n`demo-local`, and Pull request planned, built, passed its checks, opened\nPR #3 on `ianparo/bastion-demo` and merged it. The run found that the\nsandbox stops a command after five minutes, so the CI step waits four and\na half and then asks for a retry; GitHub's own CI for the PR failed, and\nthe step was shipped anyway to merge. It also found that a failed store\ntransaction was silent, that a deleted workflow's next version collided\nwith its kept ones, and that a result said again empty dropped the first;\nall three are fixed.\n\n### S3. Everything names steps\n\n- Governance per step (D42), dials per step only, notices and the job\n  page by step id, evals by step.\n- Exit, live: a read-only step's write is refused and noted in the audit.\n\nDone 2026-11-11, in part. An agent's step can only read: the gate refuses\nits file writes, and the seeded planning and criteria steps read only. A\nnotice names a workflow's step. Live: a read-only prompt told to write\n`NOTES.md` on `demo-local` was refused, and the audit log reads \"deny\n(This step only reads: it may not write a file.) sandbox::fs::write","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"07a9e211-f503-4177-99fa-4ae9b533467a","timestamp":"2026-11-11T21:44:09.103Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"# Bastion\n\nBastion is a software factory you can govern. A request goes in, agents work\nit through a workflow, and a reviewed change comes out. A workflow is a graph\nof steps (Bastion's own stages, skills, prompts and commands) that you see on\na canvas, and each step waits for your approval or runs on its own, as you set\nit. Bastion is a server and its clients: `bastion-server` runs the factory on\nthe [iii](https://iii.dev) engine, and the desktop app (on\n[iced](https://iced.rs)) and the `bastion` command line talk to it over HTTP.\nIt's early. [VISION.md](VISION.md) says where it is going, and\n[DECISIONS.md](DECISIONS.md) holds the goals and the decisions behind it.\n\n## Install\n\nOn an Apple Silicon Mac or on Linux, install the server and the CLI, and add\n`--desktop` for the app:\n\n```sh\ncurl -fsSL https://tacoda.github.io/bastion/install.sh | sh -s -- --desktop\n```\n\nOn a Mac, Homebrew works too: `brew install --cask tacoda/bastion/bastion`.\nThe installer checks each download against the release's signed checksums,\nand `bastion update` moves you to the newest release. On a Linux VM, add\n`--service` to run the server as a systemd user service.\n\n## Run it from source\n\n```sh\ncargo build -p bastion-server\ncargo run -p bastion-desktop\n```\n\nThe client starts a local server if none runs on port 49190, and the server\nstarts the stack in `worker-compose.yaml`. Go to **Engine** to see Doctor and\nthe stack, then **Jobs** and **New request** to start work.\n\n## What it is not\n\nNot an editor: the only code you see in Bastion is the diff a job proposes.\nNot a web app. Not Windows, because sandbox VMs need Apple Silicon or Linux\nwith `/dev/kvm`. A server listens on loopback unless you give it a TLS\ncertificate, and then several people can share it, each with a token the\nserver issued them.\n\n## What you need\n\n- Rust, from `rust-toolchain.toml` (rustup picks it up).\n- `iii` 0.25.0 on your `PATH`.\n- A model provider key, set in **Settings**, which keeps it in the stack's\n  vault. A key in the environment works too.\n- `gh`, signed in, for pushes and pull requests. Landing locally works\n  without it.\n\nIf something is missing, Doctor on the Engine page names it and says how to\nfix it. A failed required check stops the start.\n\n## How it fits together\n\n| Part | Where | Does |\n|---|---|---|\n| Core | `crates/bastion-core` | The factory's rules, as pure functions. |\n| Protocol | `crates/bastion-protocol` | The API's types and routes, shared by the server and its clients. |\n| Server | `crates/bastion-server` | The HTTP API and its events, the factory, the store, the gate, the audit and the stack. |\n| Engine adapter | `crates/bastion-server/src/engine/` | The only server code that imports `iii-sdk`. |\n| Client library | `crates/bastion-client` | What every client uses to reach a server: the server list, the local server's start, the calls and the event stream. |\n| Desktop client | `crates/bastion-desktop` | The pages, on iced. |\n| Command line | `crates/bastion-cli` | `bastion`: list and follow jobs, submit a request, approve, send back, stop, update. |\n| Workers | `workers/` | Bastion's own iii workers. |\n\n`scripts/check-seams.sh` fails the build when code crosses a seam (DECISIONS.md\nD19).\n\nWhat the engine was found to do, and on which versions, is in\n`docs/engine-findings.md`.\n\n## Checks\n\n```sh\ncargo fmt --all -- --check\nscripts/check-seams.sh\ncargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings\ncargo test --workspace\n```\n\nCI runs the same on macOS and Linux. The live checks need a running stack:\n`cargo test -p bastion-server -- --ignored --nocapture`.\nEach phase passes CI, is checked live, and is committed before the next.\n\n### S1. Steps run on their own\n\n- Shell steps ask for the network and name secrets (D40); the factory\n  makes the VM to match.\n- A job whose workflow has only the five kinds runs to its end, then\n  finishes by the workspace's setting (D41). The landing check that wants a\n  land stage accepts such a workflow.\n- The workspace gets its finish setting: merge, or leave the branch.\n- Exit, live: a workflow of a planning prompt, a building prompt, a test\n  shell step and a network shell step that opens a pull request on\n  `ianparo/bastion-demo`.\n\nDone 2026-11-11. On `demo-gh`, a workflow of a planning prompt, a\nbuilding prompt, a test shell step and a network shell step ran to its\nend. Plan gave its `plan` result, build committed, the test step failed\non the repository's own broken test and was shipped anyway, and the\nnetwork step pushed with `GH_TOKEN` from the vault and opened PR #2 on\n`ianparo/bastion-demo`. The job then finished, and the workspace left its\nbranch. The run found that an approved finish left the job waiting; that\nis fixed. The test workflow's own script misread GitHub's reply.\n\n### S2. The seeded workflows are steps\n\n- Each seeded workflow becomes the five kinds, with its text filled in.\n- The plan and build prompts start from today's briefs, and review is a\n  prompt. The pull request, the CI wait and the merge are `gh` shell scripts.\n- \"Runs as\" lists only the five kinds.\n- Exit, live: the seeded short workflow lands a change on `demo-local`, and\n  the seeded pull request workflow opens and merges a pull request.\n\nDone 2026-11-11. Short, Pull request, Full and the full workflow without\nacceptance tests ship as steps, saved at startup while no workflow has\ntheir id; the eleven older seeded workflows turn into steps while their\nfirst version is unchanged. Live: Short landed `negate(x)` on\n`demo-local`, and Pull request planned, built, passed its checks, opened\nPR #3 on `ianparo/bastion-demo` and merged it. The run found that the\nsandbox stops a command after five minutes, so the CI step waits four and\na half and then asks for a retry; GitHub's own CI for the PR failed, and\nthe step was shipped anyway to merge. It also found that a failed store\ntransaction was silent, that a deleted workflow's next version collided\nwith its kept ones, and that a result said again empty dropped the first;\nall three are fixed.\n\n### S3. Everything names steps\n\n- Governance per step (D42), dials per step only, notices and the job\n  page by step id, evals by step.\n- Exit, live: a read-only step's write is refused and noted in the audit.\n\nDone 2026-11-11, in part. An agent's step can only read: the gate refuses\nits file writes, and the seeded planning and criteria steps read only. A\nnotice names a workflow's step. Live: a read-only prompt told to write\n`NOTES.md` on `demo-local` was refused, and the audit log reads \"deny\n(This step only reads: it may not write a file.) sandbox::fs::write","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"serverClassifierContext":{"request":"c38ab191-a455-4603-b92d-2b4079120fd8","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","root":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":11,"modified":54,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":3},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":null,"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[],"visibility_cache":[]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3c895744-0007-416e-bef9-61c8b4a810c7","session_id":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/tacoda/tacoda/bastion","sessionId":"4fe51ecd-d6cc-423f-a39e-68f5e5b7e45e","version":"2.1.285","gitBranch":"main","slug":"parsed-tinkering-blum"}
